// Stories for the matrix: STORIES["<civ id>:<activity>"] = "…". See cultures/README.md.
Object.assign(STORIES, {
  // ── Egyptians ──
  "egyptians:hunt": "In the papyrus marshes of the Delta, a nobleman stands in a light reed skiff, throwing stick raised, his wife and daughter beside him and a trained cat leaping at the birds. Nebamun (𓎟𓇋𓏠𓈖) had this scene painted in his Theban tomb around 1350 BCE. Hunting fowl and hippopotamus in the reeds was more than sport: it was order defeating chaos, a joy the dead hoped to keep forever.",
  "egyptians:battle": "Kadesh, 1274 BCE. Ramesses II (𓇳𓄟𓋴𓇓), young and overconfident, camps ahead of his army when the Hittite chariots of Muwatalli burst across the Orontes and scatter the Division of Ra (𓂋𓂝𓇳𓀭). The pharaoh rallies his guard and charges again and again until the Ptah (𓊪𓏏𓎛) division arrives. Neither side truly won, but Ramesses carved his lone stand on temple walls from Abu Simbel (أبو سمبل) to Karnak (الكرنك), the hero of his own poem.",
  "egyptians:gather": "When the flood withdraws, the black earth of Kemet (𓆎𓅓𓏏𓊖) lies wet and soft. Farmers scatter emmer and barley and drive sheep across the fields to tread the seed in. By spring the scribes arrive with measuring cords to count the harvest and set the tax. Reapers sing work songs as their flint sickles cut, and the first sheaf is offered to Renenutet (𓂋𓈖𓈖𓅱𓏏𓏏𓆗), the cobra goddess of the granary.",
  "egyptians:ritual": "At Thebes (𓌀𓏏𓊖), during the Beautiful Festival of the Valley, priests carry the barque of Amun (𓇋𓏠𓈖) across the river on their shoulders. People write questions on ostraca, a yes and a no, and wait as the god's boat dips toward one answer. Should I trust this man? Will my son recover? The oracle of Amun decided land disputes and appointed officials; the silence before the boat moves is the holiest moment.",
  "egyptians:rain": "Rain almost never falls on Egypt; what everyone waits for is the river. In early summer the Dog Star Sopdet (𓋴𓊪𓂧𓏏𓇼) rises with the sun before dawn, and priests at the nilometer of Elephantine read the stone steps every day. Will the water rise to sixteen cubits, enough for plenty? Too little means hunger; too much, ruin. The whole land holds its breath for Hapi (𓎛𓂝𓊪𓈗).",
  "egyptians:flood": "The inundation is no disaster here but the year's great blessing. Hapi (𓎛𓂝𓊪𓈗), plump and blue-green, crowned with papyrus, pours the river over the fields and leaves behind the black silt that gives Kemet (𓆎𓅓𓏏𓊖) its name. Villages become islands, work stops, and people sail, feast and labor on the pharaoh's building projects. Hymns to the Nile say: when he rises, the land rejoices and every belly is glad.",
  "egyptians:wind": "Out of the southern desert comes the khamsin, the hot spring wind that blows for days. Sand blots out the sun over Thebes (𓌀𓏏𓊖), stings the eyes of fishermen and buries the edges of the fields. Egyptians linked the red desert and its storms to Seth (𓃩), the god of confusion and strength, who also stood at the prow of Ra's (𓂋𓂝𓇳𓀭) boat to spear the serpent Apep (𓂝𓄿𓊪𓊪𓆙) each night.",
  "egyptians:fire": "Imagined: a lamp tips over in a crowded quarter of Deir el-Medina (دير المدينة), the workmen's village of the royal tombs. Mudbrick walls hold, but palm-frond roofs and linen burn quickly, and neighbors pass jars of water hand to hand down the narrow lane. Afterward the scribe of the tomb records the loss on a limestone flake, and the women ask Taweret (𓏏𓄿𓅨𓂋𓏏) and Bes (𓃀𓋴) to guard the hearth.",
  "egyptians:thunder": "Thunder is rare in Egypt and it belongs to Seth (𓃩), whose voice roars in the sky. In the Tempest Stela, set up at Karnak (الكرنك) about 1550 BCE by King Ahmose (𓇹𓄟𓋴), a terrible storm sweeps Thebes (𓌀𓏏𓊖): darkness, rain, a noise louder than the crowds, tombs collapsed and bodies washed out. The king restores the temples and the land, showing that pharaoh puts the world back in order.",
  "egyptians:birth": "A mother crouches on two birth bricks painted with the face of Meskhenet (𓄟𓋴𓐍𓈖𓏏), who will also decide the child's fate. Midwives chant spells, an ivory wand carved with Taweret (𓏏𓄿𓅨𓂋𓏏) and Bes (𓃀𓋴) is drawn around the room to keep demons out. Many children did not survive their first year. But who knows what this one may become: a scribe, a priest, or one day a pharaoh like Hatshepsut (𓄂𓏏𓈙𓊪𓋴𓅱𓏏).",
  "egyptians:death": "Seventy days after death, the mummy of the scribe Ani (𓇋𓈖𓇋) stands upright at the door of his tomb. A priest in a jackal mask holds it while another touches its mouth with an adze in the Opening of the Mouth, so the dead may eat and speak again. Professional mourners throw dust on their heads and wail. In the Hall of Two Truths his heart will be weighed against Maat's (𓌳𓄿𓏏𓆄) feather.",

  // ── Babylonians ──
  "babylonians:hunt": "Imagined: Babylonian hunters wade into the reed thickets of the southern marshes, nets strung between boats to catch waterfowl and wild boar flushed by dogs. Lions still prowled the river country, and killing one was a royal duty, proof the king protected his flock. A hunter returning with a boar pays part of it to the temple, then sells the rest in the market of Babylon (𒆍𒀭𒊏𒆠).",
  "babylonians:battle": "Around 1763 BCE Hammurabi (𒄩𒄠𒈬𒊏𒁉) of Babylon (𒆍𒀭𒊏𒆠) dams the waters of the Tigris, then releases them to break the walls of Larsa (𒌓𒀕𒆠), where old King Rim-Sin (𒊑𒅎𒀭𒂗𒍪) has ruled for sixty years. One by one Eshnunna, Mari (𒈠𒊑𒆠) and Larsa fall. A small city-state becomes an empire, and on a basalt stele Hammurabi presents himself not just as a conqueror but as the shepherd who brings justice to the land.",
  "babylonians:gather": "Along the canals, date palms stand in rows, and in the season of harvest men climb the trunks with ropes to cut the heavy clusters. Hammurabi's (𒄩𒄠𒈬𒊏𒁉) laws set rules for gardeners who share the dates with owners. Barley fills the temple granaries of Marduk (𒀭𒀫𒌓), and scribes press the tallies into wet clay. A good year means bread and beer for every household in the city.",
  "babylonians:ritual": "A baru diviner slaughters a perfect lamb and opens it before the sun god Shamash (𒀭𒌓) and the storm god Adad (𒀭𒅎). He studies the liver, its marks and folds, comparing them with clay models and long tablets of omens. If this sign appears, the king will triumph. Kings did not march, build or marry without asking. The lamb's liver was a tablet on which the gods wrote.",
  "babylonians:rain": "In southern Mesopotamia rain is scarce, and life depends on canals fed by the Euphrates. Farmers watch the river rise with the snowmelt from distant mountains, and astronomers on the ziggurat watch the stars, writing every night in their Astronomical Diaries: the moon, the planets, the weather, the price of barley. Waiting for change, they recorded the sky for over six centuries.",
  "babylonians:flood": "On the eleventh tablet of the Epic of Gilgamesh (𒀭𒄑𒉋𒂵𒎌), Utnapishtim tells how the gods decided to drown humankind. Ea (𒀭𒂍𒀀) whispered a warning through a reed wall: tear down your house, build a ship. For six days and seven nights the storm raged, and even the gods cowered like dogs. When it ended, he released a dove, a swallow, then a raven that did not return.",
  "babylonians:wind": "In the Enuma Elish (𒂊𒉡𒈠𒂊𒇺), the creation poem recited at the Babylonian New Year, Marduk (𒀭𒀫𒌓) faces the sea monster Tiamat armed with seven winds: the evil wind, the whirlwind, the hurricane. When she opens her mouth to swallow him, he drives the winds into her body and splits her in two to make heaven and earth. A great storm is the god's weapon, terrible but creating order.",
  "babylonians:fire": "In 689 BCE the Assyrian king Sennacherib (𒀭𒌍𒉽𒈨𒌍𒋢), enraged at Babylon's (𒆍𒀭𒊏𒆠) rebellions, sacks the city, burns its houses and temples, and diverts the canals to flood the ruins. He boasts that he made it like a meadow. Babylonians mourned their city, and Marduk's (𒀭𒀫𒌓) statue was carried off to Assyria. A decade later Sennacherib's own son Esarhaddon (𒀭𒀸𒋩𒉽𒀸) began rebuilding it, hoping to calm the gods.",
  "babylonians:thunder": "Adad (𒀭𒅎), lord of storm, rides his bull and holds lightning forks in his hand. When thunder rolls over Babylon (𒆍𒀭𒊏𒆠), scribes consult the omen series Enuma Anu Enlil: if Adad thunders in this month, the harvest will prosper, or the enemy will come. Thunder is not random; it is a message, and the king's scholars write letters explaining what it means for him.",
  "babylonians:birth": "Babylonian mothers feared Lamashtu (𒀭𒆷𒈦𒌅), the demoness who stole infants and brought fever. Around the birthing room hung amulets of Pazuzu (𒀭𒉺𒍪𒍪), a demon with a dog's face, chosen to drive her away. The midwife calls on the goddess Ninhursag (𒀭𒊩𒌆𒄯𒊕). Will this child become a scribe, learning cuneiform at the tablet house, or a merchant on the river? The family prays it simply lives.",
  "babylonians:death": "The dead go down to the House of Dust, Irkalla (𒅕𒆗𒆷), where they eat clay and wear feathers. Babylonians buried their kin beneath the floors of their homes, and each month at the kispu ritual the family poured water and set bread for them, calling their names. A ghost without offerings wanders and troubles the living. Remembering the dead is a household duty, not a single day.",

  // ── Assyrians ──
  "assyrians:hunt": "The palace of Ashurbanipal (𒀸𒋩𒆕𒀀) at Nineveh (𒌷𒉌𒉡𒀀), around 645 BCE: lions are released from cages into an arena ringed by soldiers. The king, in his chariot, fires arrows and finishes them with a spear. The British Museum reliefs show a dying lioness dragging her paralyzed legs. It was a royal ritual: the king, protecting the land against chaos. The carvers gave the lions more pity than they perhaps intended.",
  "assyrians:battle": "In 701 BCE Sennacherib (𒀭𒌍𒉽𒈨𒌍𒋢) besieges Lachish (לכיש) in Judah (יהודה). Assyrian engineers build a siege ramp of stones; battering rams roll up it under covering archers, while defenders hurl torches down. The city falls. Reliefs at Nineveh (𒌷𒉌𒉡𒀀) show families walking into exile with their belongings. The Assyrian army was the most organized of its age, and it wanted every visitor to the palace to see this.",
  "assyrians:gather": "Imagined: in the rain-watered plains around Assur (𒀸𒋩𒆠), farmers cut barley with bronze sickles while a royal official watches to take his share for the army. Captives resettled from distant lands work beside them, speaking Aramaic. Ashurnasirpal II boasted of planting gardens at Nimrud with trees and seeds collected on his campaigns, and the harvest feeds the next season of war.",
  "assyrians:ritual": "When a lunar eclipse threatened the life of King Esarhaddon (𒀭𒀸𒋩𒉽𒀸), Assyrian scholars set a substitute king on the throne: a commoner given royal robes, while the real king hid as a farmer. For up to a hundred days the omen could fall on the stand-in. Letters to the king show his fear and the scholars' careful reassurance that the danger would pass.",
  "assyrians:rain": "Northern Assyria relies on rain, not canals. When the winter rains fail, the grain withers, and letters from provincial governors reach the king: no rain has fallen, the people are hungry. Some researchers think a long drought helped weaken the empire in its last decades. The priests ask Adad (𒀭𒅎) for rain; the farmers watch the clouds come over the Zagros and pass.",
  "assyrians:flood": "In 612 BCE the Medes and Babylonians besiege Nineveh (𒌷𒉌𒉡𒀀). A later tradition, echoed in the book of Nahum (נחום), says the river gates were opened and the palace dissolved. Whether a flood breached the walls or not, Nineveh fell that year and the great empire collapsed within a generation. For the Assyrians, it was an end of the world.",
  "assyrians:wind": "Imagined: a dust storm rolls out of the Syrian steppe while an Assyrian army is on the march. Soldiers wrap their faces, horses turn their tails to the wind, and the chariot column halts. The scribe who keeps the campaign record will not mention it; Assyrian annals tell only of victory. But each soldier remembers the sting of sand and the long day without sight.",
  "assyrians:fire": "Nineveh (𒌷𒉌𒉡𒀀) burns in 612 BCE. The palace of Ashurbanipal (𒀸𒋩𒆕𒀀) collapses, and the fire bakes hard the clay tablets of his great library, thousands of them, including the Epic of Gilgamesh (𒀭𒄑𒉋𒂵𒎌). The empire died in that blaze, but its words were preserved by it, waiting in the ruins until Layard and Rassam dug them out in the 1840s and 1850s.",
  "assyrians:thunder": "Imagined: thunder rolls over Assur (𒀸𒋩𒆠) during a campaign season, and a soldier on the walls asks his officer whether Adad (𒀭𒅎) is angry. The palace scholars are already writing to the king: if Adad thunders when the moon is in a certain sign, the king's enemy will be defeated. The Assyrians trusted thunder as long as experts were there to read it.",
  "assyrians:birth": "Imagined: in a house in Nineveh (𒌷𒉌𒉡𒀀), a mother in labor clutches an amulet of Pazuzu (𒀭𒉺𒍪𒍪) while the midwife recites an incantation against Lamashtu (𒀭𒆷𒈦𒌅). The father, a soldier, is away on campaign with the king. Will the child be a scribe, a merchant, or another soldier? The family hopes for a son, and the mother simply hopes to live to see it grow.",
  "assyrians:death": "Royal Assyrian funerals followed ancient rites: the king's body was laid in a stone sarcophagus in the old capital of Assur (𒀸𒋩𒆠), with offerings of oil, food and precious goods. A text describes King Esarhaddon's (𒀭𒀸𒋩𒉽𒀸) mourners and the gifts sent with him. The ghost must be fed through the kispu offerings by descendants, or it would wander, hungry and dangerous.",

  // ── Sumerians ──
  "sumerians:hunt": "Imagined: at dawn in the marshes near Ur (𒋀𒀊𒆠), Sumerian fishermen pole their reed boats through the channels, spearing carp and setting nets for ducks. The city's temple of Nanna (𒀭𒋀𒆠) takes its share of every catch, recorded on clay. Older hunters still tell how Gilgamesh (𒀭𒄑𒉋𒂵𒎌) and Enkidu (𒀭𒂗𒆠𒄭) went to the Cedar Forest and killed its guardian, Humbaba (𒄷𒉿𒉿), and wonder whether the gods truly forgave them.",
  "sumerians:battle": "Around 2450 BCE Eannatum (𒂍𒀭𒈾𒁺) of Lagash (𒉢𒁓𒆷𒆠) fights the neighboring city of Umma (𒄑𒆵𒆠) over a fertile strip of land called the Gu-edena (𒄘𒂔𒈾). His Stele of the Vultures shows his soldiers marching in a tight phalanx of shields and spears over the bodies of the enemy, while vultures carry off heads. The war over that boundary ditch lasted generations, the first border dispute we can read.",
  "sumerians:gather": "A Sumerian text called the Farmer's Instructions, written about 1700 BCE, is a father's advice to his son: watch the flood, open the canal gates carefully, keep oxen ready, harvest the barley before it falls over. Reapers work in teams of three. Then the threshing floor, the winnowing, and a prayer to Ninkasi, goddess of beer, for there is barley enough to brew.",
  "sumerians:ritual": "Each year at Ur (𒋀𒀊𒆠), the moon god Nanna (𒀭𒋀𒆠) receives offerings of fat sheep, dates and butter at his ziggurat. Enheduanna (𒂗𒃶𒌌𒀭𒈾), daughter of Sargon (𒊬𒊒𒄀) of Akkad (𒀀𒂵𒉈𒆠), served as high priestess there around 2300 BCE and wrote hymns to Inanna (𒀭𒈹), the first author known by name. Priests watch the moon's horns and the flight of birds and ask whether the year will be good. The answer is sought in silence.",
  "sumerians:rain": "In Sumer (𒆠𒂗𒂠) the sky seldom rains; the land waits on the Tigris and Euphrates. In the Lament for Ur (𒋀𒀊𒆠), the storm of Enlil (𒀭𒂗𒆤) sweeps the city and there is no rain for the fields, no grass for the herds. Salt slowly crept into the fields as irrigation went on for centuries. Farmers switched from wheat to barley, waiting for a better time that did not come.",
  "sumerians:flood": "In the Sumerian story of Ziusudra (𒍣𒌓𒋤𒁺), the gods decide to send a flood. Enki (𒀭𒂗𒆠) warns the pious king, and for seven days and seven nights the flood sweeps over the land while a great boat is tossed on the waters. Then Utu (𒀭𒌓), the sun god, shines in. Ziusudra bows before him and is granted eternal life. The Sumerian King List even divides history into before and after the flood.",
  "sumerians:wind": "The Lament for Ur (𒋀𒀊𒆠), written around 2000 BCE, mourns a great storm, Enlil's (𒀭𒂗𒆤) storm, which destroyed the city: the Elamites came like a wind, and the dead lay in the streets like molten bronze. The goddess Ningal (𒀭𒊩𒌆𒃲) pleads with the gods to stop it, but the storm does not cease. The poem is the voice of a whole city grieving for itself.",
  "sumerians:fire": "Imagined: a fire sweeps through the reed houses outside the walls of Uruk (𒌷𒀕) on a dry summer night. Reed burns like straw, and within an hour the whole quarter is ash. Neighbors bring jars of water from the canal, a priest calls on Gibil, god of fire, to be calm. By morning they are cutting fresh reeds from the marsh to build again, as their ancestors did.",
  "sumerians:thunder": "Iškur (𒀭𒅎), the Sumerian storm god, rides the clouds and roars over the mountains. His thunder can bring the rain that the northern hills need, or hail that ruins the crops. In hymns he is a wild bull. Imagined: a farmer outside Lagash (𒉢𒁓𒆷𒆠) hears him rumble and looks to the east, unsure whether Iškur brings a blessing or a threat.",
  "sumerians:birth": "The goddess Ninhursag (𒀭𒊩𒌆𒄯𒊕), the mother of the land, shapes children in the womb. A Sumerian incantation compares the child in labor to a boat carrying precious cargo, asking the gods to bring it safely to the quay. Many mothers and infants died. Yet the family imagines a scribe who will learn the wedge script, or perhaps another Gilgamesh (𒀭𒄑𒉋𒂵𒎌), two-thirds god.",
  "sumerians:death": "At Ur (𒋀𒀊𒆠) around 2500 BCE, Queen Puabi was buried with gold leaves in her hair, lyres, carts and dozens of attendants who seem to have died with her, laid out in rows in the Great Death Pit excavated by Leonard Woolley. For ordinary people, burial was simpler: a grave under the floor, a cup and a plate, so they might not go hungry in the dark underworld.",

  // ── Hittites ──
  "hittites:hunt": "Imagined: on the high Anatolian plateau, Hittite huntsmen ride out with hawks and dogs after deer and wild sheep. On a relief at Alaca Höyük, a hunter faces a stag and a lion attacks a bull. A stag was sacred to the tutelary god of the countryside, and the hunter leaves part of each kill at a rock shrine in thanks.",
  "hittites:battle": "Kadesh, 1274 BCE, from the Hittite side. King Muwatalli II lures Ramesses II with false spies, then sends some 2,500 chariots across the river into the Egyptian camp. The trap nearly works. Sixteen years later Hattusili III (𒄩𒀜𒌅𒅆𒇷) and Ramesses sign the first known peace treaty, a copy of which hangs today at the United Nations. Both sides claimed glory, but peace held.",
  "hittites:gather": "In the uplands around Hattusa (𒌷𒄩𒀜𒌅𒊭), farmers harvest emmer and barley before the short summer ends. Vineyards ripen on the slopes, and great grain silos at Hattusa could store food for thousands. The Hittite laws list prices for an ox, a sheep, a measure of wine. A harvest festival honors Telipinu (𒀭𒋼𒇷𒉿𒉡), the god who once vanished in anger and took growth with him.",
  "hittites:ritual": "Hittite priests asked the gods yes-or-no questions using oracles: the flight of birds, the entrails of sheep, and the KIN (𒆥) oracle, a game of symbols moved by an old woman. King Mursili II (𒈬𒌨𒅆𒇷), during a plague around 1320 BCE, asked again and again why the gods were angry, and wrote his prayers down: what sin of my father must I atone for? Each answer was recorded.",
  "hittites:rain": "The myth of Telipinu (𒀭𒋼𒇷𒉿𒉡) tells of a god who disappeared in a rage. Mist covered the windows, smoke filled the house, grain no longer grew, cattle and people stopped giving birth. The gods searched everywhere until a bee found him asleep in a meadow and stung him awake. A ritual brought him home. The Hittites knew what it meant to wait for the world to come back to life.",
  "hittites:wind": "Imagined: a storm sweeps down from the Pontic mountains over Hattusa (𒌷𒄩𒀜𒌅𒊭), tearing at the roofs of the Great Temple and scattering clay tablets kept in the storerooms. Priests ask the oracles whether the Storm God of Hatti (𒌷𒄩𒀜𒋾) is angry. Old men say it is Teshub fighting the dragon Illuyanka (𒈲𒅋𒇻𒅀𒀭𒅗) again, just as the festival of Purulli (𒁍𒊒𒌌𒇷) tells each spring.",
  "hittites:fire": "Around 1180 BCE Hattusa (𒌷𒄩𒀜𒌅𒊭) was abandoned and burned. Archaeologists found the destroyed great temple and citadel. Was it invasion, drought, or the court leaving first? The capital of an empire that had matched Egypt went dark, and Hittite writing vanished. Thousands of tablets baked in the ruins kept their laws and prayers for archaeologists to find over 3,000 years later.",
  "hittites:thunder": "The Storm God of Hatti (𒌷𒄩𒀜𒋾), Tarhunna (𒀭𒋻𒄷𒌦𒈾), rides a chariot drawn by bulls named Serri and Hurri. He is the king of the gods, and the king of Hatti is his steward. Thunder over Hattusa (𒌷𒄩𒀜𒌅𒊭) means the god is present. In the Purulli (𒁍𒊒𒌌𒇷) festival, priests recite how he killed the serpent Illuyanka (𒈲𒅋𒇻𒅀𒀭𒅗) with the help of the goddess Inara (𒀭𒄿𒈾𒊏), ensuring the land would prosper.",
  "hittites:birth": "Hittite birth rituals, recorded on tablets from Kizzuwatna (𒌷𒆠𒄑𒍪𒉿𒀜𒈾), describe a birthing stool, a midwife who speaks spells, and offerings to the Mother Goddesses who spin the fate of the newborn. The family waits outside the room. Will this child become a scribe at the palace, a soldier, a priestess? The goddesses already know; the family can only hope.",
  "hittites:death": "When a Hittite king died, people said he had become a god. For fourteen days his body was burned, his bones gathered with silver tongs and soaked in oil, wrapped in fine cloth and laid on a chair in a stone house. Offerings were made each day, an image of the king was honored, and the court mourned in a precise order that tablets preserve.",

  // ── Phoenicians ──
  "phoenicians:hunt": "Imagined: on the cedar-covered slopes above Byblos (𐤂𐤁𐤋), Phoenician hunters chase deer and wild goats, while on the shore below their cousins hunt a different prey: the murex sea snail. Thousands of shells are crushed to make Tyrian purple, a dye worth more than gold. The workshops stink of rotting shellfish, but kings across the sea will wear the color.",
  "phoenicians:battle": "In 332 BCE Tyre (𐤑𐤓), an island city, refused to open its gates to Alexander (Ἀλέξανδρος) the Great. For seven months Alexander's engineers built a causeway through the sea while Tyrians sent fire ships against it and fought from their walls. When the city fell, thousands were killed and tens of thousands enslaved. The causeway remains: Tyre is no longer an island.",
  "phoenicians:gather": "Imagined: in the hills behind Sidon (𐤑𐤃𐤍), families harvest olives and grapes, pressing oil and wine to load into amphoras for the ships. The Phoenicians were merchants first, so every harvest becomes trade goods. A father teaches his son to count with the new alphabet, the one that will spread to Greece and beyond: aleph (𐤀), beth (𐤁), gimel (𐤂).",
  "phoenicians:ritual": "Before a ship sails, the captain brings a gift to the temple of Melqart (𐤌𐤋𐤒𐤓𐤕) in Tyre (𐤑𐤓), or to Astarte (𐤏𐤔𐤕𐤓𐤕). Herodotus (Ἡρόδοτος) visited the temple and saw two pillars, one of gold and one of emerald glowing at night. Sailors ask: will the voyage to Gadir (𐤂𐤃𐤓) return safely? Small terracotta figures and lamps left at shrines along the coast record their quiet requests.",
  "phoenicians:rain": "Imagined: in Ugarit (𐎜𐎂𐎗𐎚), a city on the same coast, the myth of Baal (𐤁𐤏𐤋) told how the storm god was swallowed by Mot (𐤌𐤕), Death, and for a time no rain fell. Phoenician farmers knew the dry summer was a season of waiting. When the first autumn clouds came over the sea, people said Baal had returned from the underworld.",
  "phoenicians:flood": "Lucian (Λουκιανός), writing in the 2nd century CE, describes it: when rains swell the Adonis (Ἄδωνις) River near Byblos (𐤂𐤁𐤋), its waters run red with iron-rich soil from the mountains. People said it was the blood of Adonis, killed by a boar, and women mourned him in the Adonia (Ἀδώνια) festival. The flood is both a grief and a renewal: the god returns with the spring.",
  "phoenicians:wind": "Imagined: a Phoenician ship out of Tyre (𐤑𐤓), laden with cedar and purple cloth, is caught in a winter gale off Cyprus. The crew lowers the sail and throws cargo overboard, praying to Baal (𐤁𐤏𐤋) and Melqart (𐤌𐤋𐤒𐤓𐤕). Shipwrecks like those near Ashkelon (𐤀𐤔𐤒𐤋𐤍) and Mazarrón show many did not return. Those who reach port bring offerings and tell the story for years.",
  "phoenicians:fire": "Imagined: in the harbor of Sidon (𐤑𐤃𐤍) a ship's tar catches fire, and the flames leap from deck to deck along the crowded quay. Sailors cut mooring lines and push burning ships into the open water. The glassmakers and dyers come running. Phoenicians lived by the sea, and losing a fleet meant losing a season's trade.",
  "phoenicians:thunder": "Baal Hadad (𐤁𐤏𐤋 𐤄𐤃𐤃), the storm god, rides the clouds and hurls lightning. In the Ugaritic poems he builds a palace with a window, and when it is opened his voice, the thunder, shakes the earth and makes the mountains tremble. Phoenician sailors heard thunder as the voice of the god who could save or sink them.",
  "phoenicians:birth": "Imagined: a merchant's wife in Byblos (𐤂𐤁𐤋) gives birth while her husband is away on a voyage to Carthage (𐤒𐤓𐤕𐤇𐤃𐤔𐤕) or Gadir (𐤂𐤃𐤓). The midwife calls on Astarte (𐤏𐤔𐤕𐤓𐤕), and a small amulet of the Egyptian dwarf god Bes hangs over the bed. Will the child be a sailor, a scribe, or a founder of a new city like Elissa, who founded Carthage?",
  "phoenicians:death": "In 1923 archaeologists found the sarcophagus of Ahiram (𐤀𐤇𐤓𐤌), king of Byblos (𐤂𐤁𐤋), around 1000 BCE. Its lid carries one of the earliest Phoenician alphabet inscriptions: a curse on anyone who disturbs the tomb. Mourning women are carved on its side, tearing their hair and beating their breasts. Phoenicians grieved loudly and wanted their dead to rest in peace.",

  // ── Palmyrans ──
  "palmyrans:hunt": "Imagined: Palmyrene archers ride out from the oasis into the Syrian steppe after gazelle and wild ass. They were famous bowmen, later serving Rome (Roma) from Dacia to Britain (Britannia). A young hunter brings his first gazelle home through the colonnaded street and offers a portion to Bel (𐡡𐡫) at the great temple, as his father did before him.",
  "palmyrans:battle": "In 272 CE Queen Zenobia (𐡡𐡶𐡦𐡡𐡩), who had made Palmyra (𐡶𐡣𐡬𐡴) an empire stretching from Egypt to Anatolia, faced Emperor Aurelian (Aurelianus) near Antioch (Ἀντιόχεια) and then at Emesa (Ἔμεσα). Her heavy cavalry, the clibanarii, charged and were drawn into exhaustion by the Roman retreat. Zenobia fled toward Persia on a camel but was caught at the Euphrates (Εὐφράτης). Her defiance is still celebrated in Syria.",
  "palmyrans:gather": "The spring of Efqa (𐡠𐡱𐡳𐡠) watered gardens of palms, olives and pomegranates in the middle of the desert. But Palmyra's (𐡶𐡣𐡬𐡴) true harvest was trade: caravans from the Euphrates (Εὐφράτης) brought silk, spices and pearls. A tax law of 137 CE, carved in Greek and Aramaic, lists dues on everything from slaves to perfumed oil. Gathering, here, meant counting camels.",
  "palmyrans:ritual": "On the feast of the god Bel (𐡡𐡫), priests at the great temple dedicated in 32 CE hold a banquet. Members of the brotherhood eat together on couches, entering with small clay tokens as tickets. Divination and prayer ask Bel, Yarhibol (𐡩𐡴𐡧𐡡𐡥𐡫) and Aglibol (𐡰𐡢𐡫𐡡𐡥𐡫), gods of the sun and moon, whether the caravans will return safely. The temple stood almost two thousand years, until 2015.",
  "palmyrans:rain": "Palmyra (𐡶𐡣𐡬𐡴) lived on its oasis spring and on rain-fed cisterns in the surrounding hills. Imagined: in a dry year the caravan leaders gather in the agora to decide whether to send camels across the desert when the wells may be dry. They wait for the clouds, and for word from the Euphrates (Εὐφράτης), before risking everything.",
  "palmyrans:wind": "Imagined: a dust storm rises from the desert and engulfs the caravan city. The colonnades vanish in yellow air, camels kneel and lower their heads, and merchants shelter in the caravanserais. The storm can last for days and bury a caravan on the open road. Caravan leaders, honored on statues along the colonnade, earned their fame by bringing travelers through such storms.",
  "palmyrans:fire": "After Zenobia's (𐡡𐡶𐡦𐡡𐡩) defeat, Palmyra (𐡶𐡣𐡬𐡴) rebelled again in 273 CE. Aurelian (Aurelianus) returned, sacked the city and burned parts of it. The temple of the sun was looted for treasure. Palmyra never regained its glory. Its citizens, once lords of the caravan route, watched their wealth reduced to ash, and the city slowly became a small desert garrison.",
  "palmyrans:thunder": "Imagined: thunder rolls over the desert on a rare winter night, and rain fills the cisterns and wadis around the oasis. Palmyrenes thank Baalshamin (𐡡𐡰𐡫𐡵𐡬𐡭), the lord of the heavens, whose temple stood near the colonnade. Desert people know a storm can bring both flash floods and a year's water, and they greet it with awe.",
  "palmyrans:birth": "Imagined: a girl is born in a merchant house of Palmyra (𐡶𐡣𐡬𐡴), and an old aunt says that Zenobia (𐡡𐡶𐡦𐡡𐡩) too was born here, the daughter of a local family, and became a queen who challenged Rome (Roma). The mother laughs, exhausted. The father thinks of the risks: fever, the desert, wars. Still, the family offers incense to Bel (𐡡𐡫) and dreams.",
  "palmyrans:death": "Palmyrenes buried their dead in tall tower tombs and underground hypogea outside the city, each body in a niche sealed with a carved portrait. The faces look out calmly, wearing jewelry and naming themselves in Aramaic: alas, so-and-so, son of so-and-so. Many of these busts survive in museums. Families gathered at banquets in the tombs to remember them.",

  // ── Minoans ──
  "minoans:hunt": "Imagined: in the mountains of Crete (Κρήτη), young men hunt the agrimi, the wild goat with long curved horns, using bows and dogs. Its horns were prized for making composite bows. The famous bull-leaping frescoes of Knossos (𐀒𐀜𐀰) show a different contest: youths somersaulting over a charging bull, a dance with the most dangerous animal on the island.",
  "minoans:battle": "The Minoans left surprisingly few images of war, and their palaces had no great walls; perhaps their fleet was their defense. Imagined: Minoan ships drive off raiders near Crete (Κρήτη), the crew rowing hard and loosing arrows. Around 1450 BCE, Mycenaean Greeks took over Knossos (𐀒𐀜𐀰), and Minoan Crete gradually became a Greek world.",
  "minoans:gather": "At Knossos (𐀒𐀜𐀰), great clay jars called pithoi, taller than a person, stored olive oil, wine and grain. The Harvester Vase from Hagia Triada (Αγία Τριάδα) shows a procession of farmers with tools on their shoulders, singing, led by a man shaking a sistrum. It is one of the most joyful scenes in ancient art: the harvest as a festival.",
  "minoans:ritual": "On peak sanctuaries like Mount Juktas (Γιούχτας), Minoans lit bonfires and left small clay figures of people and animals, perhaps asking healing or protection. In 1979 archaeologists at Anemospilia (Ανεμόσπηλια) found a temple destroyed by an earthquake around 1700 BCE, with what seemed to be a young man laid on an altar. What the priests asked the gods in that final moment is unknown.",
  "minoans:rain": "Imagined: in a dry summer on Crete (Κρήτη), farmers climb to a peak sanctuary to leave clay figurines and light fires, asking the goddess for rain. The olive trees endure, but grain withers. They look south to the sea and north to the clouds over Thera (Θήρα), waiting. A century later, the change they did not imagine would come from that direction.",
  "minoans:flood": "Around 1600 BCE the volcano of Thera (Θήρα), Santorini, erupted in one of the largest eruptions of human history. The town of Akrotiri (Ακρωτήρι) was buried, though its people seem to have fled in time. Tsunamis may have struck the north coast of Crete (Κρήτη), and the ash spread across the Aegean (Αἰγαῖον). Some see in this the seed of Plato's (Πλάτων) story of Atlantis (Ἀτλαντίς).",
  "minoans:wind": "Imagined: a great storm drives across the Aegean (Αἰγαῖον), and Minoan ships returning from Egypt with ivory and gold race for the harbor of Kommos (Κομμός). The sails tear and rowers strain against the waves. The Minoans were masters of the sea, and their frescoes of ships at Akrotiri (Ακρωτήρι) show them proud, but every family knew the sea could take its sons.",
  "minoans:fire": "Around 1450 BCE fire swept through the palaces of Crete (Κρήτη). At Knossos (𐀒𐀜𐀰) the flames baked the clay tablets written in Linear B, the script of the Mycenaean Greeks who then ruled there, preserving their inventories of sheep, oil and slaves. The palaces at Phaistos (𐀞𐀂𐀵) and Zakros (Ζάκρος) burned too. Whether by war or disaster, an era ended in flames.",
  "minoans:thunder": "Imagined: thunder rolls over Mount Ida (Ἴδη), and Cretans say the young god has been born again in the cave. Later Greeks believed Zeus (Ζεύς) was hidden as an infant in a Cretan cave, while warriors called Kouretes (Κουρῆτες) clashed their shields so his father would not hear him cry. Perhaps the Minoans already heard a god in the storm.",
  "minoans:birth": "Imagined: a mother in a Cretan villa gives birth with the help of women of her family, while a clay figurine of a goddess holding snakes watches over the house. Minoan art shows women as priestesses and leaders. The family wonders whether this girl might serve at the palace sanctuary, or leap bulls in the court at Knossos (𐀒𐀜𐀰).",
  "minoans:death": "At Hagia Triada (Αγία Τριάδα) a painted stone sarcophagus from about 1400 BCE shows a funeral: women pour offerings into a vessel between double axes, a man plays a lyre, a bull lies sacrificed on a table, and the dead man stands before his tomb receiving gifts of a boat and animals. It is one of the clearest glimpses of how Minoans honored their dead.",

  // ── Greeks ──
  "greeks:hunt": "In the Odyssey (Ὀδύσσεια), young Odysseus (Ὀδυσσεύς) hunts a great boar on Mount Parnassus (Παρνασσός) with his grandfather's sons. The boar charges and gashes his leg above the knee before he kills it. Decades later, the scar lets his old nurse Eurycleia (Εὐρύκλεια) recognize him when he returns home in disguise. For Greeks (Ἕλληνες), the hunt tested a young man and left marks that told his story.",
  "greeks:battle": "Marathon (Μαραθών), 490 BCE. About ten thousand Athenians (Ἀθηναῖοι) and Plataeans (Πλαταιεῖς) face the Persian army on the plain. The general Miltiades (Μιλτιάδης) thins his center and strengthens his wings; the hoplites run the last stretch under arrow fire, and the wings close around the Persians. 192 Athenians die and are buried in the mound that still stands there. Aeschylus (Αἰσχύλος), who fought there, was proudest of that day.",
  "greeks:gather": "Hesiod's (Ἡσίοδος) Works and Days (Ἔργα καὶ Ἡμέραι), written around 700 BCE, is a farmer's calendar: when the Pleiades (Πλειάδες) rise, begin the harvest; when they set, plow. He warns his lazy brother Perses (Πέρσης) that the gods hid livelihood from humans, and only work brings it. In autumn the olive harvest, in late summer the grapes trodden in the vat, and a cup of wine in the shade.",
  "greeks:ritual": "At Delphi (Δελφοί), the Pythia (Πυθία) sits on a tripod in the temple of Apollo (Ἀπόλλων) and speaks the god's answer. In 480 BCE the Athenians (Ἀθηναῖοι), terrified of the Persian invasion, were told that a wooden wall would save them. Themistocles (Θεμιστοκλῆς) argued that the wall meant their ships. They took to the sea and won at Salamis (Σαλαμίς). People came to Delphi for advice on wars, colonies and marriages.",
  "greeks:rain": "In dry years Greek farmers prayed to Zeus (Ζεύς) for rain. Marcus Aurelius preserved the Athenian prayer: rain, rain, dear Zeus, on the fields of the Athenians (Ἀθηναῖοι). Farmers watched for the clouds to gather over Mount Hymettus (Ὑμηττός), a sign that the rains would come. The whole agricultural year waited on the autumn rains, which fell just in time or not at all.",
  "greeks:flood": "Deucalion (Δευκαλίων) and Pyrrha (Πύρρα), the Greek flood story: Zeus (Ζεύς), disgusted by human wickedness, floods the world. Deucalion, warned by his father Prometheus (Προμηθεύς), builds a chest and floats for nine days until he lands on Mount Parnassus (Παρνασσός). Told to throw the bones of their mother behind them, they throw stones, which become new people. Greeks (Ἕλληνες) pointed to the place on Parnassus where they landed.",
  "greeks:wind": "In the Odyssey (Ὀδύσσεια), the wind god Aeolus (Αἴολος) gives Odysseus (Ὀδυσσεύς) a bag holding all the storm winds, leaving only the gentle west wind to carry him home. Within sight of Ithaca (Ἰθάκη), his crew opens the bag, thinking it holds gold. The winds burst out and blow them back across the sea. A storm, for Greeks (Ἕλληνες), could come from the gods, or from one's own foolishness.",
  "greeks:fire": "In 356 BCE a man named Herostratus (Ἡρόστρατος) burned the Temple of Artemis (Ἄρτεμις) at Ephesus (Ἔφεσος), one of the Seven Wonders, simply to make his name famous. The Ephesians (Ἐφέσιοι) condemned him and forbade anyone to speak his name, but historians recorded it anyway. Legend says the fire burned the night Alexander the Great (Ἀλέξανδρος ὁ Μέγας) was born, while Artemis was away attending the birth.",
  "greeks:thunder": "Zeus (Ζεύς) the thunderer, whose lightning bolts were forged by the Cyclopes (Κύκλωπες). A place struck by lightning became sacred, fenced off and left untouched. When thunder rolled over Olympia (Ὀλυμπία), Greeks (Ἕλληνες) felt the god was present, and great festivals there honored him. In the Iliad (Ἰλιάς), Zeus thunders from Mount Ida (Ἴδη) to signal which side he favors. The sound was a message.",
  "greeks:birth": "A Greek birth was attended by a midwife and the women of the household, with prayers to Artemis (Ἄρτεμις) and Eileithyia (Εἰλείθυια). On the fifth or seventh day the child was carried around the hearth in the Amphidromia (Ἀμφιδρόμια), and on the tenth day named. Euripides' (Εὐριπίδης) Medea (Μήδεια) says she would rather stand three times in battle than bear one child. Yet who knew whether this boy might become a Pericles (Περικλῆς), or a Socrates (Σωκράτης).",
  "greeks:death": "The body is washed, dressed and laid out at home for the prothesis, women lamenting around it and tearing their hair. Then before dawn the ekphora: the procession to the cemetery, with flutes and mourners. A coin for Charon (Χάρων) is placed in the mouth to pay the ferry across the Styx (Στύξ). Painted vases show the scene. Tombs were visited with oil and ribbons for generations.",

  // ── Athenians ──
  "athenians:hunt": "Xenophon (Ξενοφῶν), an Athenian who fought with the Ten Thousand, wrote the Cynegeticus (Κυνηγετικός), a book on hunting hares with hounds. He describes how the dogs pick up the scent at dawn, the nets spread across the paths, the hunters running through the hills of Attica (Ἀττική). For him the hunt trained young men for war and for virtue: patience, endurance, and quick judgment.",
  "athenians:battle": "Salamis (Σαλαμίς), September 480 BCE. The Persians (𐎱𐎠𐎼𐎿) have burned the Acropolis (Ἀκρόπολις), and the Athenians (Ἀθηναῖοι) watch from their ships as smoke rises over their city. Themistocles (Θεμιστοκλῆς) lures the Persian fleet into the narrow strait, where its numbers become a weakness. Greek triremes ram and shear oars. Aeschylus (Αἰσχύλος), who fought there, later wrote The Persians (Πέρσαι), imagining the defeat through the eyes of the enemy.",
  "athenians:gather": "The olive was Athena's (Ἀθηνᾶ) gift to Athens (Ἀθῆναι), given when she won the contest with Poseidon (Ποσειδῶν) for the city. Sacred olive trees, the moriai (μορίαι), stood in the countryside, and their oil filled the prize amphoras of the Panathenaic Games (Παναθήναια). Harvest was hard work, beating the branches with sticks. Cutting down a sacred olive could bring exile or death.",
  "athenians:ritual": "Every autumn, thousands walked the Sacred Way (Ἱερὰ Ὁδός) from Athens (Ἀθῆναι) to Eleusis (Ἐλευσίς) for the Mysteries of Demeter (Δημήτηρ) and Persephone (Περσεφόνη). Initiates were sworn to secrecy, and so faithfully kept it that we still do not know what they saw. Cicero, an initiate, said they learned to live with joy and to die with better hope. A quiet awe surrounded the whole procession.",
  "athenians:rain": "In 430 BCE, during the war with Sparta (Σπάρτη), Athens (Ἀθῆναι) was crowded with refugees from the countryside, and a plague broke out. Thucydides (Θουκυδίδης), who caught it and survived, described the fever, the thirst, the bodies piled in temples. Pericles (Περικλῆς) himself died. The Athenians (Ἀθηναῖοι) waited for the war, the plague, and their fortunes to change. Their world never quite returned.",
  "athenians:flood": "Imagined: a winter storm sends the Kephisos (Κηφισός) and Ilissos (Ἰλισσός) rivers over their banks, flooding the low fields of Attica (Ἀττική) and the edges of the city. Farmers lose their seed, and potters in the Kerameikos (Κεραμεικός) haul their wares to higher ground. Athenians (Ἀθηναῖοι) recalled that Poseidon (Ποσειδῶν), angry at losing the city to Athena (Ἀθηνᾶ), had once flooded the Thriasian plain (Θριάσιον πεδίον).",
  "athenians:wind": "On the Roman-era Tower of the Winds in the Athenian agora, eight winds are carved as flying figures: Boreas (Βορέας), the cold north wind, blowing a conch; Zephyros (Ζέφυρος), scattering flowers. Athenians (Ἀθηναῖοι) honored Boreas after a storm wrecked part of the Persian fleet in 480 BCE, believing the north wind, their in-law through Oreithyia (Ὠρείθυια), had answered their prayers.",
  "athenians:fire": "In 480 BCE the Persians (𐎱𐎠𐎼𐎿) under Xerxes (𐎧𐏁𐎹𐎠𐎼𐏁𐎠) captured Athens (Ἀθῆναι) and burned the temples on the Acropolis (Ἀκρόπολις). The Athenians (Ἀθηναῖοι), watching from Salamis (Σαλαμίς), swore not to rebuild them, as a memorial. Thirty years later Pericles (Περικλῆς) broke that oath and raised the Parthenon (Παρθενών). Archaeologists later found the broken statues the Athenians had buried after the fire, the Persian debris.",
  "athenians:thunder": "In Aristophanes' (Ἀριστοφάνης) comedy The Clouds (Νεφέλαι), Socrates (Σωκράτης) tells a farmer that thunder is not Zeus (Ζεύς) but clouds colliding and bursting. The audience laughed, but the play reflected a real anxiety: philosophers questioning the gods. In 399 BCE Socrates was tried for impiety and corrupting the youth, and he drank the hemlock. Thunder was a question of faith in Athens (Ἀθῆναι).",
  "athenians:birth": "On the tenth day after birth, Athenian families held a feast to name the child, often after a grandfather. An olive wreath on the door meant a boy, a tuft of wool a girl. Pericles (Περικλῆς) was born around 495 BCE; his mother Agariste (Ἀγαρίστη) dreamed she gave birth to a lion. No one knew whether a newborn would lead Athens (Ἀθῆναι) or die of a fever before spring.",
  "athenians:death": "Every winter during the Peloponnesian War (Πελοποννησιακὸς πόλεμος), Athens (Ἀθῆναι) held a public funeral for those killed in battle. The bones were laid in cypress coffins, one for each tribe, with an empty bier for the missing. In 431 BCE Pericles (Περικλῆς) gave the Funeral Oration (Ἐπιτάφιος) recorded by Thucydides (Θουκυδίδης): the whole earth is the tomb of famous men. Mourning became a statement of what the city was.",

  // ── Spartans ──
  "spartans:hunt": "Spartan boys in the agoge were underfed on purpose and encouraged to steal food; being caught meant a beating. Plutarch (Πλούταρχος) tells of a boy who hid a stolen fox under his cloak and let it gnaw his belly rather than reveal it, dying in silence. Hunting with the famous Laconian hounds trained them for war and fed the common messes.",
  "spartans:battle": "Thermopylae (Θερμοπύλαι), August 480 BCE. King Leonidas (Λεωνίδας) and three hundred Spartans (Σπαρτιᾶται), with several thousand allies, hold the narrow pass against Xerxes (𐎧𐏁𐎹𐎠𐎼𐏁𐎠). For two days the Persian waves break on the Greek shields. Then a local man, Ephialtes (Ἐφιάλτης), shows the Persians (𐎱𐎠𐎼𐎿) a mountain path. Leonidas sends most allies away and fights to the last. The epitaph: go tell the Spartans, passerby, that here, obedient to their laws, we lie.",
  "spartans:gather": "Spartan citizens did not farm; the helots (εἵλωτες) did. These enslaved Messenians (Μεσσήνιοι) and Laconians (Λάκωνες) worked the fields and handed over a fixed share of barley, wine and oil to their Spartan masters. Each year the ephors formally declared war on the helots, a sign of how afraid Spartans (Σπαρτιᾶται) were of them. The harvest fed the soldiers but rested on fear.",
  "spartans:ritual": "Spartans (Σπαρτιᾶται) were famous for their piety: before every battle the king sacrificed and read the omens, and they would not march during sacred festivals. At Plataea (Πλάταιαι) in 479 BCE, the omens were bad and the Spartans stood under arrow fire, waiting, until the king Pausanias (Παυσανίας) prayed to Hera (Ἥρα) and the signs turned favorable. Only then did they charge.",
  "spartans:rain": "In 490 BCE Athens (Ἀθῆναι) begged Sparta (Σπάρτη) for help against the Persians (𐎱𐎠𐎼𐎿) at Marathon (Μαραθών). The Spartans (Σπαρτιᾶται) agreed, but said they could not march until the full moon, during the Carneia (Κάρνεια) festival. They arrived the day after the battle, inspected the Persian dead and praised the Athenians (Ἀθηναῖοι). Spartan waiting was a matter of religion, even when the world could not wait.",
  "spartans:flood": "Imagined: the Eurotas (Εὐρώτας) river, swollen by winter rains from Mount Taygetus (Ταΰγετος), floods its banks below Sparta (Σπάρτη), where boys of the agoge have their reed beds. The boys pull reeds from the water with their bare hands, as they always did, without knives. For Spartans (Σπαρτιᾶται) even the river's flood was another test of endurance.",
  "spartans:wind": "Imagined: on the slopes of Taygetus (Ταΰγετος) a winter storm tears through the training grounds, and Spartan boys, given one cloak a year and no shoes, huddle together in silence. Their instructors watch. Complaining would shame them. Plutarch (Πλούταρχος) says Spartan boys learned to endure cold and hunger so that nothing in war would surprise them.",
  "spartans:fire": "Imagined: lightning strikes a temple on the acropolis of Sparta (Σπάρτη), and the citizens rush to fight the fire while priests consult the omens. Sparta had no walls, proud that its soldiers were its walls, but fire was an enemy that could not be fought with spears. The elders ask the oracle at Delphi (Δελφοί) which god is angry.",
  "spartans:thunder": "In 464 BCE a great earthquake struck Sparta (Σπάρτη), destroying much of the city and, it was said, killing many of its young men. The helots (εἵλωτες) revolted. The Spartans (Σπαρτιᾶται), struggling, asked Athens (Ἀθῆναι) for help, then sent the Athenian troops home in suspicion, beginning the rift that would lead to the Peloponnesian War (Πελοποννησιακὸς πόλεμος). The earth roared like thunder.",
  "spartans:birth": "According to Plutarch (Πλούταρχος), a newborn Spartan was brought before the elders, who inspected it. If it was healthy, it would be raised; if weak, it was said to be left at the Apothetae (Ἀποθέται), a place near Mount Taygetus (Ταΰγετος). Modern historians doubt the extent of this. Mothers, it was said, bathed newborns in wine to test their strength. Hope and fear came together at the cradle.",
  "spartans:death": "Spartan law, Plutarch (Πλούταρχος) says, allowed the name of the dead on a tombstone only for men who died in war and women who died in childbirth. Mourning was short, eleven days, and restrained. When a king died, however, the whole land mourned loudly, with helots (εἵλωτες) forced to wail. A mother told her son: come back with your shield, or on it.",

  // ── Macedonians ──
  "macedonians:hunt": "At Vergina, the tomb believed to be Philip II's (Φίλιππος Β΄) has a painted frieze of a royal hunt: horsemen and men on foot with spears chase a lion, a boar and a bear through the forest, and a young man, perhaps Alexander (Ἀλέξανδρος), rides at the center. In Macedonia (Μακεδονία) a man could not recline at the banquet until he had killed a boar without a net.",
  "macedonians:battle": "Gaugamela (Γαυγάμηλα), October 331 BCE. Alexander (Ἀλέξανδρος) faces Darius III (𐎭𐎠𐎼𐎹𐎺𐎢𐏁) and a vast Persian army on a plain chosen for chariots. He leads his Companion cavalry (Ἑταῖροι) to the right, opening a gap in the Persian line, and drives straight at Darius, who flees. The Persian Empire falls. Alexander was twenty-five, and his soldiers would follow him as far as India.",
  "macedonians:gather": "Imagined: in the green valleys of Macedonia (Μακεδονία), below Mount Olympus (Ὄλυμπος), farmers harvest wheat and gather grapes while timber cutters fell pine and fir for ships, Macedonia's greatest export. Many sons of these families are away in Asia with Alexander (Ἀλέξανδρος). The harvest is brought in by old men, women and children, who send letters asking when they will come home.",
  "macedonians:ritual": "In 332 BCE Alexander (Ἀλέξανδρος) made a dangerous journey through the Libyan desert to the oracle of Ammon at Siwa. The chief priest greeted him, by some accounts, as son of Zeus-Ammon (Ζεὺς Ἄμμων). Alexander never revealed what he asked; he wrote to his mother Olympias (Ὀλυμπιάς) that he would tell her in person. He died before he could.",
  "macedonians:rain": "In 326 BCE, on the banks of the Hyphasis (Ὕφασις) river in India, the Macedonian army, after eight years of marching, refused to go further. The monsoon rains had fallen for seventy days, armor rusted, and the men longed for home. Alexander (Ἀλέξανδρος) sulked in his tent for three days, waiting for them to change their minds. They did not. He turned back.",
  "macedonians:flood": "Imagined: the Axios (Ἀξιός) river floods the plain near Pella (Πέλλα) after heavy rains, and the royal capital's harbor lake overflows. Fields of wheat lie underwater. Macedonians (Μακεδόνες) were used to river valleys; this was a hard year, but the young king Philip (Φίλιππος) will soon build drainage works and fortifications, transforming his poor kingdom into the strongest in Greece (Ἑλλάς).",
  "macedonians:wind": "In 325 BCE Alexander (Ἀλέξανδρος) led part of his army back from India through the Gedrosian Desert (Γεδρωσία). Arrian (Ἀρριανός) tells of a flash flood from a sudden storm that swept away the camp, wives and children, and of burning winds and sandstorms. Thousands died of thirst and heat. It was the cruelest march of the campaign, and Alexander shared every hardship.",
  "macedonians:fire": "In 330 BCE, at Persepolis (Περσέπολις), the ceremonial capital of Persia, Alexander's (Ἀλέξανδρος) men burned the palace of Xerxes (𐎧𐏁𐎹𐎠𐎼𐏁𐎠) after a drunken feast. Some say it was revenge for the burning of Athens (Ἀθῆναι); the Athenian courtesan Thais (Θαΐς) urged it on. Plutarch (Πλούταρχος) says Alexander soon regretted it. The charred ruins still stand in Iran.",
  "macedonians:thunder": "Plutarch (Πλούταρχος) says that on the night before her wedding to Philip (Φίλιππος), Olympias (Ὀλυμπιάς) dreamed a thunderbolt struck her womb and a great fire spread from it. Philip later dreamed he sealed her womb with a lion's seal. Seers said she was pregnant with a son as brave as a lion. Alexander (Ἀλέξανδρος) grew up believing he might be the son of Zeus (Ζεύς).",
  "macedonians:birth": "Alexander (Ἀλέξανδρος) was born in Pella (Πέλλα) in July 356 BCE. On the same day, Plutarch (Πλούταρχος) says, Philip (Φίλιππος) received three pieces of news: his general Parmenion (Παρμενίων) had beaten the Illyrians (Ἰλλυριοί), his horse had won at Olympia (Ὀλυμπία), and his son was born. The seers said a son born amid three victories would be invincible. No one guessed how far he would go, or how young he would die.",
  "macedonians:death": "When Alexander (Ἀλέξανδρος) died in Babylon in June 323 BCE, aged thirty-two, his generals quarreled over the body. A magnificent funeral cart took two years to build. Ptolemy (Πτολεμαῖος) hijacked it and brought it to Egypt; the tomb in Alexandria (Ἀλεξάνδρεια) was visited by Caesar and Augustus, but its location is lost. A year earlier, grieving his friend Hephaestion (Ἡφαιστίων), Alexander himself had ordered the manes of horses shorn in mourning.",

  // ── Romans ──
  "romans:hunt": "In 80 CE Emperor Titus opened the Flavian Amphitheatre (Amphitheatrum Flavium), the Colosseum, with a hundred days of games; some 9,000 animals were killed in the venationes. Lions, bears, elephants and ostriches were hunted in front of fifty thousand spectators. Animals were captured across the empire, from Mesopotamia to Mauretania. Some Romans (Romani), like Cicero, found the slaughter tiresome; most of the crowd roared.",
  "romans:battle": "Cannae, 2 August 216 BCE. Hannibal (𐤇𐤍𐤁𐤏𐤋) lets his center bend back while his African infantry and cavalry close around the Roman legions from both sides. Some fifty thousand Romans (Romani) die in a single afternoon, among them a consul and eighty senators. Rome (Roma) does not surrender. The Senate (Senatus) forbids public mourning beyond thirty days, raises new legions, and fights on for fourteen years.",
  "romans:gather": "Cato the Elder's (Cato Maior) De Agri Cultura, written around 160 BCE, gives instructions for running a farm: how to press olives, how to make wine, what to feed slaves when they are sick (less). At Rome (Roma), grain from Egypt and Sicily (Sicilia) arrived by ship at Ostia, and the annona fed the city's poor. Harvest festivals honored Ceres, and the Saturnalia ended the year in feasting.",
  "romans:ritual": "Before any public act, Roman augurs watched the sky: the flight of birds, the behavior of the sacred chickens. In 249 BCE the consul Publius Claudius Pulcher, before the naval battle of Drepana (Δρέπανα), was told the chickens would not eat. Furious, he threw them into the sea: let them drink, then. He lost his fleet. Romans (Romani) remembered the lesson for centuries.",
  "romans:rain": "On 24 August 79 CE, the sky darkened over Pompeii as Vesuvius erupted. Pliny the Younger (Plinius Minor), watching from Misenum across the bay, described a cloud shaped like an umbrella pine and the people waiting, unsure whether to flee or stay. His uncle Pliny the Elder (Plinius Maior) sailed to rescue friends and died on the beach at Stabiae. The ash fell like snow, and the world did not wait.",
  "romans:flood": "The Tiber (Tiberis) flooded Rome (Roma) again and again. In 15 CE, Tacitus says, the river swelled and filled the low parts of the city, collapsing buildings and drowning people. The Senate (Senatus) debated diverting its tributaries, but towns upstream protested that the gods had set the rivers where they were. Romans (Romani) learned to live with the Tiber's moods; marks on churches still show flood levels.",
  "romans:wind": "In 255 BCE, after a victory against Carthage (𐤒𐤓𐤕𐤇𐤃𐤔𐤕), a Roman fleet of over 300 ships was caught in a storm off Camarina (Καμάρινα) in Sicily (Sicilia). The consuls, ignoring the pilots' warnings, hugged the coast, and most of the fleet was smashed on the rocks. Tens of thousands drowned. It was one of the worst maritime disasters in antiquity; the Romans (Romani) built a new fleet.",
  "romans:fire": "On the night of 18 July 64 CE a fire broke out near the Circus Maximus and burned for six days. Of Rome's fourteen districts, three were destroyed and seven badly damaged. Nero, away at Antium, returned and opened his gardens to the homeless. Rumors said he sang of the fall of Troy (Troia) as the city burned. Tacitus says he blamed the Christians.",
  "romans:thunder": "Romans (Romani) read lightning carefully: a place struck by a thunderbolt was sacred, enclosed and called a bidental. Priests interpreted the direction and the timing. In 44 BCE omens before Caesar's assassination included storms and strange lights in the sky. His wife Calpurnia dreamed of disaster. Caesar went to the Senate (Senatus) anyway, and on the Ides of March (Idus Martiae) he died.",
  "romans:birth": "A newborn Roman was laid on the ground before the father, who could lift it up to accept it into the family. On the ninth day for boys, the eighth for girls, the dies lustricus, the child was purified, named, and given a bulla amulet to wear. Gaius Octavius, born in 63 BCE in a small house on the Palatine (Palatium), would become Augustus. No one could foresee that.",
  "romans:death": "When a Roman noble died, the funeral procession wound to the Forum. Actors wore the wax masks of his ancestors, imagines, so that the dead consuls and generals walked again with him. His son gave a eulogy from the rostra. Polybius (Πολύβιος) found it the most inspiring sight a young man could see. Then the body was burned outside the city walls.",

  // ── Carthaginians ──
  "carthaginians:hunt": "Carthage (𐤒𐤓𐤕𐤇𐤃𐤔𐤕) captured wild elephants in the forests of North Africa and trained them for war. Imagined: hunters in the Atlas foothills drive a herd into a stockade, choose young animals, and walk them slowly toward the city's great stables in the walls, which held three hundred elephants. A captured elephant was a soldier worth a hundred men.",
  "carthaginians:battle": "In 218 BCE Hannibal (𐤇𐤍𐤁𐤏𐤋) crosses the Alps (Alpes) with some 40,000 men and 37 elephants, losing many to snow, cliffs and mountain tribes. Polybius (Πολύβιος) says he showed his starving soldiers the plains of Italy (Italia) from the pass to give them hope. Then came victories at the Trebia, Lake Trasimene (Lacus Trasimenus) and Cannae. Hannibal spent fifteen years in Italy and never took Rome (Roma).",
  "carthaginians:gather": "The Carthaginian Mago (𐤌𐤂𐤍) wrote a famous treatise on agriculture in 28 books. After Carthage (𐤒𐤓𐤕𐤇𐤃𐤔𐤕) fell in 146 BCE, the Roman Senate (Senatus) ordered it translated into Latin, the only Carthaginian books spared. Carthaginians farmed olives, grapes, figs and pomegranates on rich estates in Tunisia. Cato showed a fresh Carthaginian fig in the Senate to warn how close and prosperous the enemy was.",
  "carthaginians:ritual": "Carthaginians worshiped Baal Hammon (𐤁𐤏𐤋 𐤇𐤌𐤍) and Tanit (𐤕𐤍𐤕), and before great undertakings consulted the gods with offerings. At the sanctuary archaeologists call the tophet, thousands of small urns of burned infant bones were buried with stelae dedicated to Tanit. Scholars still debate whether they were child sacrifices, as Greek and Roman writers claimed, or a cemetery for infants who died young.",
  "carthaginians:rain": "After the First Punic War in 241 BCE, Carthage (𐤒𐤓𐤕𐤇𐤃𐤔𐤕) had to pay a huge indemnity to Rome (Roma), and its unpaid mercenaries revolted. For three years the city waited, besieged by its own former soldiers. Polybius (Πολύβιος) called it the most savage war he knew. Carthaginians endured, waiting for Hamilcar Barca (𐤇𐤌𐤋𐤒𐤓𐤕 𐤁𐤓𐤒) to crush the revolt, which at last he did.",
  "carthaginians:flood": "Imagined: a winter storm sends the Bagradas river over its banks, flooding the farms on the plain below Carthage (𐤒𐤓𐤕𐤇𐤃𐤔𐤕). Farmers move their families to the hills and wait for the waters to fall. Later the river silted up and changed its course, leaving Utica (𐤏𐤕𐤒), once a port, far from the sea. Carthaginians knew rivers rearranged the land.",
  "carthaginians:wind": "Imagined: a Carthaginian trading ship rounds the Pillars of Heracles (Ἡρακλέους στῆλαι), heading south along the coast of Africa as Hanno (𐤇𐤍𐤀) the Navigator did around 500 BCE. A storm from the Atlantic strikes. The crew prays to Tanit (𐤕𐤍𐤕) and Baal (𐤁𐤏𐤋), while the steersman keeps her before the wind. The Periplus of Hanno told of fires on the shores and hairy people the explorers called gorillas.",
  "carthaginians:fire": "Spring 146 BCE. After a three-year siege, Scipio Aemilianus storms Carthage (𐤒𐤓𐤕𐤇𐤃𐤔𐤕). Fighting rages for six days through the streets around the Byrsa hill, the houses set ablaze. The surviving 50,000 Carthaginians are enslaved. Polybius (Πολύβιος), standing beside Scipio, saw him weep as the city burned, quoting Homer (Ὅμηρος): a day will come when sacred Troy (Ἴλιος) shall perish. He was thinking of Rome (Roma).",
  "carthaginians:thunder": "Imagined: thunder rolls over the Gulf of Tunis as the Carthaginian fleet sits in the circular military harbor, the cothon, where some 220 warships could be housed. From the admiral's island tower, lookouts watch the sky. Thunder over the sea means the gods are speaking; a priest at the temple of Baal Hammon (𐤁𐤏𐤋 𐤇𐤌𐤍) is asked what it means for the war.",
  "carthaginians:birth": "Hannibal (𐤇𐤍𐤁𐤏𐤋) was born around 247 BCE, son of Hamilcar Barca (𐤇𐤌𐤋𐤒𐤓𐤕 𐤁𐤓𐤒). Livy (Livius) says that at age nine, before going to Spain with his father, he was made to swear at an altar never to be a friend of Rome (Roma). Imagined: in a house in Carthage (𐤒𐤓𐤕𐤇𐤃𐤔𐤕) a new child is born, and the family wonders if it will grow up to carry such an oath, or live in peace.",
  "carthaginians:death": "Carthaginians buried their dead in rock-cut tombs and shafts on the hills around the city, with lamps, amulets, razors and small masks to frighten demons. In the necropolis archaeologists found masks with grimacing faces and stelae of priests and priestesses. A 2016 study of a young man's skeleton from Byrsa hill found European ancestry, a reminder of the city's wide world.",

  // ── Aztecs ──
  "aztecs:hunt": "During the month of Quecholli (Quechōlli), the Mexica (Mēxihcah) held a great ceremonial hunt on the Hill of Zacatepec in honor of Mixcoatl (Mixcōātl), the Cloud Serpent, god of the hunt. Hunters made arrows for days, then formed a long line and drove deer, rabbits and coyotes across the slopes. Sahagún's informants describe the hunters returning with the game and the honors given to those who caught the most.",
  "aztecs:battle": "On the night of 30 June 1520, the Spaniards (españoles) and their Tlaxcalan (Tlaxcaltēcah) allies tried to slip out of Tenochtitlan (Tenōchtitlan) along the Tlacopan (Tlacōpan) causeway. A woman fetching water saw them and raised the alarm. Mexica (Mēxihcah) warriors in canoes attacked from both sides; hundreds of Spaniards and thousands of allies drowned or died, weighed down by gold. The Spaniards called it the Noche Triste. The Mexica called it a victory.",
  "aztecs:gather": "On the chinampas, the floating gardens of Lake Texcoco (Tetzcohco), families harvest maize, beans, squash, chili and amaranth. The great market at Tlatelolco (Tlatelōlco), which amazed Bernal Díaz, was full of produce, turkeys, cacao and obsidian. During the harvest festivals of Huey Tozoztli (Huēyi Tōzōztli), young women carried maize ears to the temple of Chicomecoatl (Chicōmecōātl), the maize goddess, to bless the seed for the next year.",
  "aztecs:ritual": "Every 52 years, when the two calendars returned to the same day, the Mexica (Mēxihcah) feared the world might end. All fires were extinguished. On the night of the New Fire, priests climbed the Hill of the Star and watched for the Pleiades (tianquiztli) to cross the zenith. When they did, a fire was kindled on a captive's chest and carried by runners to every hearth. The last was held in 1507.",
  "aztecs:rain": "The Mexica (Mēxihcah) waited anxiously each spring for Tlaloc (Tlāloc), the rain god, to send the rains. The drought and famine of 1454, One Rabbit (Ce Tōchtli), was remembered for generations: people sold themselves into slavery to the Totonacs for maize. In the festivals of Atlcahualo (Ātlcahualo), offerings to the tlaloque (tlālohqueh), the rain spirits, were made on the mountains. When the rains came, the whole valley exhaled.",
  "aztecs:flood": "Around 1500 the ruler Ahuitzotl (Āhuitzotl) built an aqueduct from the springs of Coyoacán (Coyōhuahcān) to Tenochtitlan (Tenōchtitlan), ignoring a warning. The water came so strong that it flooded the city. Houses collapsed, chinampas were drowned, and Ahuitzotl himself was said to have hit his head escaping. Nezahualcoyotl (Nezahualcoyōtl) of Texcoco (Tetzcohco) had earlier built a great dike to protect the city from the lake.",
  "aztecs:wind": "Ehecatl (Ehēcatl), the wind god, an aspect of Quetzalcoatl (Quetzalcōātl), sweeps the road for the rain gods with his breath. His round temples let the wind circle without corners. In the Legend of the Suns, the second world, the Sun of Wind, ended when hurricanes destroyed everything and its people became monkeys. A great storm reminded the Mexica (Mēxihcah) that worlds could end.",
  "aztecs:fire": "In the years before the Spanish arrived, the Mexica (Mēxihcah) recorded strange omens. Among them, the temple of Huitzilopochtli caught fire by itself; the more water the people threw on it, the more it burned. A comet appeared in daylight. A woman was heard crying at night: my children, we must go far away. Sahagún's informants remembered these as signs of the end.",
  "aztecs:thunder": "Tlaloc (Tlāloc) rules from Tlalocan (Tlālōcān), the paradise of the rain gods. Thunder is the sound of his helpers, the tlaloque (tlālohqueh), striking their water jars. People killed by lightning or drowning were not cremated but buried, and went to Tlalocan, a place of eternal spring. A thunderstorm over the lake was frightening, but also a sign of the rain that fed the maize.",
  "aztecs:birth": "When a Mexica (Mēxihcah) child was born, the midwife shouted war cries: the mother had fought a battle and captured a prisoner. A mother who died in childbirth was honored as a warrior, the mocihuaquetzqueh. The midwife told a baby boy that his true home was the battlefield. The child's name depended on the day sign in the tonalpohualli (tōnalpōhualli) calendar; diviners read whether its fate would be good or harsh.",
  "aztecs:death": "The Mexica (Mēxihcah) believed the destination of the dead depended on how they died, not how they lived. Most went to Mictlan (Mictlān), a four-year journey through nine levels, guided by a small dog. The body was wrapped and burned with offerings, and the ashes kept. For the next four years families offered food on the anniversaries. Warriors who fell in battle accompanied the sun.",

  // ── Mayans ──
  "mayans:hunt": "In the Popol Vuh (Popol Wuj), the hero twins Hunahpu (Junajpu) and Xbalanque (Xb'alanke) are expert blowgun hunters. They shoot the arrogant bird-demon Seven Macaw (Wuqub' Kaqix) from his tree, knocking out his teeth. Maya hunters in the forests of Petén went after deer, peccary and turkey with spears and blowguns, asking permission from the lords of the animals before entering the forest.",
  "mayans:battle": "In 562 CE the city of Caracol (Uxwitza'), allied with powerful Calakmul, defeated mighty Tikal (Yax Mutul) in a star war, a battle timed by the planet Venus. Tikal's king Wak Chan K'awiil was probably captured and sacrificed, and for over a century no monuments were raised there. Then in 695 Tikal's king Jasaw Chan K'awiil defeated Calakmul. Maya wars were long rivalries between great families.",
  "mayans:gather": "In the Popol Vuh (Popol Wuj), the gods tried to make humans from mud and wood, failing each time, until they ground yellow and white maize into dough and shaped the first true people. Maya farmers still plant milpas of maize, beans and squash. At harvest, families offer the first ears to the earth and to the ancestors. For the Maya, maize is flesh, not just food.",
  "mayans:ritual": "In Maya kingdoms, the king himself drew his own blood to speak with the ancestors. On Yaxchilan (Pa' Chan) Lintel 24, from 709 CE, Lady K'ab'al Xook pulls a thorned rope through her tongue while King Itzamnaaj Bahlam holds a torch above her. In the next lintel, a vision serpent rises from the smoke of blood-stained paper and an ancestor emerges from its mouth to speak.",
  "mayans:rain": "Between about 800 and 1000 CE, the great Maya cities of the southern lowlands were abandoned one by one. Lake sediments show a series of severe droughts. Kings who had claimed to bring rain through Chaak could no longer do so. Imagined: a farmer at Copán waits in the dry season, watching clouds that do not break, and decides to move north with his family.",
  "mayans:flood": "In the Popol Vuh (Popol Wuj), the gods destroy the wooden people, who had no hearts or minds, with a great flood of black rain. Their animals and pots turn on them, their dogs speak, and those who survived became monkeys. Then the gods tried again, making humans from maize. The flood was a cleansing, a step toward a better creation.",
  "mayans:wind": "The Maya word hurakan, from the K'iche' god Huracán (Juraqan), the one-legged creator and storm, gave us the word hurricane. In the Popol Vuh (Popol Wuj) he is the Heart of Sky (Uk'u'x Kaj), who sent the flood. Each autumn hurricanes from the Caribbean sweep the Yucatán. Imagined: a village near Tulum shelters in stone buildings while Huracán passes, and fishermen pull their canoes far from the shore.",
  "mayans:fire": "Imagined: in the dry season, a Maya farmer burns his cleared plot before planting, as Maya farmers do still. The smoke rises over the forest, and a shaman, an aj q'ij, makes an offering to the earth before the burn. Fire was part of the cycle: from the ashes, the maize will grow. In 1562 the friar Diego de Landa burned Maya books at Maní, and the Maya wept.",
  "mayans:thunder": "Chaak, the rain god, strikes the clouds with his axe of lightning to release the rain. His long-nosed masks cover the buildings of Uxmal and Kabah. Even today, in Yucatán, farmers hold the Ch'a Chaak ceremony, asking for rain with an offering of maize drink, while boys tied to the altar legs croak like frogs. Thunder is Chaak coming to the fields.",
  "mayans:birth": "In Palenque (Lakamha'), the inscriptions of the Temple of the Cross tell how K'inich Janaab' Pakal was born in 603 CE. He took the throne at twelve and ruled for 68 years. Imagined: a Maya family brings a newborn to a day-keeper, who reads its birth day in the 260-day calendar. Will the child be a ruler, a scribe, or a farmer? The calendar offers clues.",
  "mayans:death": "In 1952 Alberto Ruz opened a hidden staircase in the Temple of the Inscriptions at Palenque (Lakamha') and found the tomb of K'inich Janaab' Pakal, who died in 683 CE. He lay in a great sarcophagus wearing a jade mask, its lid carved with him falling into, or rising from, the jaws of the underworld. Ordinary Maya were buried under their house floors, close to the family.",

  // ── Incas ──
  "incas:hunt": "The Inca (Inka) held a great royal hunt called the chaku. Thousands of men formed a human circle many kilometers wide and slowly closed it, driving vicuñas, guanacos and deer into the middle. Vicuñas were sheared and released; their fine wool belonged to the Inca. Predators were killed and some deer taken for meat. The chaku is still practiced in Peru today.",
  "incas:battle": "On 16 November 1532 at Cajamarca, the Sapa Inca (Sapa Inka) Atahualpa (Atawallpa), fresh from victory over his brother Huáscar (Waskar), entered the plaza carried on a litter with thousands of unarmed attendants. Friar Valverde handed him a breviary, which he threw to the ground. Pizarro's 168 men opened fire with guns and cannon, charged with horses, and seized Atahualpa. Thousands of Andeans were killed in a single afternoon.",
  "incas:gather": "In the Inca (Inka) empire, the harvest belonged to three: the gods, the Inca, and the community. Terraces climbed the mountainsides, and freeze-dried potatoes, chuño (ch'uñu), were stored in thousands of qollqa storehouses along the roads. In May, the festival of Aymoray (Aymuray) celebrated the maize harvest, with songs and the corn carried home. The Inca himself plowed the first furrow in the sacred field.",
  "incas:ritual": "Capacocha (Qhapaq Hucha): in a time of crisis or for a new ruler, chosen children from across the empire were brought to Cusco (Qusqu), then led to the high peaks and offered to the mountain gods. In 1999 three were found on Llullaillaco, near 6,700 meters, so well preserved they seemed asleep. Their families were honored. To the Inca (Inka) it was the highest gift, binding the empire to the apus.",
  "incas:rain": "In the Andes, the Inca (Inka) built astronomical towers and watched the Pleiades, the Collca (Qullqa), each June. If they shone bright, rains would be good; if dim, it would be dry. Modern researchers found this works: El Niño years bring high thin clouds that dim the stars. Andean farmers still watch the Pleiades in June to decide when to plant potatoes.",
  "incas:flood": "Imagined: an El Niño year brings torrential rains to the coast, and the rivers below the Andes overflow, sweeping away fields and roads. The Inca's (Inka) officials open the storehouses to feed the people. Andean myth tells of an ancient flood, the unu pachakuti, sent by Viracocha (Wiraqucha) to destroy the first giant people before he made humans anew at Lake Titicaca (Titiqaqa).",
  "incas:wind": "Imagined: a winter storm sweeps the high puna, and a chasqui (chaski) runner on the royal road fights the wind and sleet to reach the next post, a quipu (khipu) of knotted cords in his bag. The relay could carry a message 240 kilometers a day. In the tambo (tampu) he passes the message and collapses by the fire. The road must not stop.",
  "incas:fire": "In 1536 Manco Inca (Manqu Inka), who had been crowned by the Spaniards as a puppet, rebelled and besieged Cusco (Qusqu) with a huge army. His warriors hurled red-hot stones wrapped in burning cotton onto the thatched roofs, and the city burned. The Spaniards, trapped in two buildings, made a desperate charge on the fortress of Sacsayhuamán (Saksaywaman). The siege failed, and Manco retreated to Vilcabamba (Willkapampa).",
  "incas:thunder": "Illapa, the thunder god, carries a sling and a club. Thunder is the crack of his sling, lightning the flash of his clothes, and rain the water he pours from a jug held by his sister in the Milky Way. People struck by lightning and survived were believed chosen to become healers. Twins, too, were considered children of Illapa.",
  "incas:birth": "Inca (Inka) children were not named until weaned, around age two, at the rutuchikuy, the first haircut, when relatives cut locks of hair and gave gifts. The child's hair and nails were kept. Pachacuti (Pachakutiq), born around 1418, transformed a small kingdom into the largest empire in the Americas. Imagined: a mother in a village near Cusco (Qusqu) hopes her son will survive to that day.",
  "incas:death": "The Inca (Inka) kings did not truly die. Their mummies, mallquis (mallki), stayed in their palaces, attended by servants, given food and drink, consulted on decisions and carried to festivals. Each royal lineage, panaca (panaka), cared for its ancestor. The Spanish were astonished. Commoners too were placed in caves or chullpa towers, and families visited them with chicha and food on feast days.",

  // ── Haudenosaunee ──
  "haudenosaunee:hunt": "In autumn, after the harvest, Haudenosaunee (Rotinonhsión:ni) men left the longhouse villages for the hunting grounds, often for months, tracking white-tailed deer through the forests of what is now New York. Hunters gave thanks to the animal's spirit and shared the meat. The Thanksgiving Address (Ohén:ton Karihwatéhkwen), spoken at the opening of gatherings, gives thanks to the animals who still offer themselves as food.",
  "haudenosaunee:battle": "In 1779 George Washington ordered the Sullivan Expedition against the Haudenosaunee (Rotinonhsión:ni) nations allied with Britain. American troops burned over forty towns, orchards and fields of corn, beans and squash. Many families fled to Fort Niagara and spent a terrible winter there. Washington became known among the Seneca (Onöndowá'ga:) as Hanodagá:nyas, Town Destroyer, a name remembered still.",
  "haudenosaunee:gather": "The Three Sisters, corn, beans and squash, grow together in the same mound: the corn gives the beans a pole to climb, the beans feed the soil, the squash shades the ground. Women owned the fields and directed the harvest, and a clan mother's word carried weight in council. At the Green Corn ceremony, the people give thanks for the ripening of the corn.",
  "haudenosaunee:ritual": "At midwinter, the Haudenosaunee (Rotinonhsión:ni) hold their longest ceremony of the year, giving thanks and renewing the cycle. Dreams are shared and interpreted, for a dream could reveal what the soul needed. Medicine societies perform their work. The Thanksgiving Address (Ohén:ton Karihwatéhkwen) is spoken, greeting the people, the earth, the waters, the plants, the animals, the winds, the moon, the sun, the stars and the Creator (Shonkwaia'tíson).",
  "haudenosaunee:rain": "Imagined: in a dry summer the corn leaves curl in the fields, and the elders gather at the longhouse to speak the Thanksgiving Address (Ohén:ton Karihwatéhkwen) and give thanks to the Thunderers (Ratiwé:ras), the grandfathers who bring the rain. People wait, watching the western sky over the lake. When the first drops fall on the bark roof, the children run out into it.",
  "haudenosaunee:flood": "Imagined: spring snowmelt swells the Genesee River, flooding the low fields near a Seneca (Onöndowá'ga:) village. Families carry seed corn to higher ground. In the Haudenosaunee (Rotinonhsión:ni) creation story, Sky Woman fell to a world of water, and animals dove to bring up mud, which they placed on the back of a turtle. The earth grew there: Turtle Island.",
  "haudenosaunee:wind": "In Haudenosaunee (Rotinonhsión:ni) tradition, the wind spirits are honored in the Thanksgiving Address (Ohén:ton Karihwatéhkwen): the Four Winds, who bring the changing seasons and cleanse the air. Imagined: a great autumn storm off Lake Ontario tears at the elm bark of the longhouses, and families move to the center hearths, waiting it out together, the old people telling stories through the night.",
  "haudenosaunee:fire": "The Haudenosaunee (Rotinonhsión:ni) Confederacy was founded when the Peacemaker and Hiawatha (Ayenwatha) brought five warring nations together under the Great Tree of Peace. The central council fire burns at Onondaga, where the Grand Council still meets. To say the fire is lit means the council is in session. A fire in the longhouse was family, and the fire at Onondaga was the whole Confederacy.",
  "haudenosaunee:thunder": "The Thunderers, Hinon, are grandfathers who live in the west and bring the rain that waters the crops. They are honored in the Thanksgiving Address. In spring, ceremonies welcome back the Thunderers when their first rumbles are heard, thanking them for the coming rains and for keeping harmful creatures under the earth.",
  "haudenosaunee:birth": "Haudenosaunee (Rotinonhsión:ni) society is matrilineal: a child belongs to its mother's clan, Turtle, Wolf, Bear or another, and clan mothers choose the chiefs. Decisions are to be made thinking of seven generations to come. Imagined: a child is born in a longhouse, and the clan mother holds it, wondering what this one will carry forward for the seventh generation.",
  "haudenosaunee:death": "When a chief dies, the Condolence Ceremony is held: the clearminded nations come to console the grieving nations, lifting their sorrow so they can see, hear and speak clearly again. The ceremony goes back to the Peacemaker, who comforted Hiawatha (Ayenwatha) after he lost his daughters. Only then is a new chief raised up. Grief is shared by the whole Confederacy, not carried alone.",

  // ── Lakota ──
  "lakota:hunt": "Before the horse, the Lakota (Lakȟóta) hunted buffalo on foot; after it, they became the great mounted hunters of the northern plains. A buffalo hunt was organized by akíčhita, the camp police, who kept anyone from charging early and scattering the herd. The tȟatȟáŋka gave meat, hides for tipis, horns for spoons, sinew for thread. Giving thanks was part of the hunt.",
  "lakota:battle": "On 25 June 1876, Lt. Col. George Custer attacked a huge camp of Lakota (Lakȟóta), Cheyenne and Arapaho on the Little Bighorn River, called the Greasy Grass (Pȟežísla Wakpá). Warriors led by Crazy Horse (Tȟašúŋke Witkó), Gall (Phizí) and others counterattacked, and Custer and his whole battalion of over 200 men were killed. Sitting Bull (Tȟatȟáŋka Íyotake) had dreamed of soldiers falling into camp. It was the high point of resistance.",
  "lakota:gather": "Lakota (Lakȟóta) women gathered tȟíŋpsiŋla, wild prairie turnips, in early summer, digging them with a sharpened stick and braiding them to dry. They picked chokecherries in late summer, pounding them with buffalo meat and fat into wasná, pemmican, to last the winter. The moon of ripe chokecherries, Čhaŋpȟásapa Wí, is a time of plenty.",
  "lakota:ritual": "The Sun Dance, Wiwáŋyaŋg Wačhípi, is the most important Lakota (Lakȟóta) ceremony, held in midsummer for the good of the people. Dancers make vows and offer their own suffering so that the people may live. The government banned it in 1883; it was held secretly and revived openly in the 20th century. A vision quest, haŋbléčheya, is crying for a vision, alone on a hill.",
  "lakota:rain": "In 1889 the Ghost Dance (Wanáǧi Wačhípi) reached the Lakota (Lakȟóta) from Wovoka, a Paiute prophet. He taught that if the people danced, the dead would return, the buffalo would come back, and the whites would vanish. Starving on reservations, their land cut by treaties, Lakota families danced and waited for the world to change. The government feared it, leading to tragedy.",
  "lakota:wind": "Imagined: a winter blizzard sweeps the plains, and the Lakota (Lakȟóta) camp shelters in its tipis in a river valley lined with cottonwoods. The fire burns low, children sleep in buffalo robes, and grandmothers tell winter stories of Iktómi, the trickster spider. The winter count, a painted hide of years, will record this as the winter of the great snow.",
  "lakota:fire": "Imagined: in late summer a grass fire sweeps across the prairie, driven by the wind, faster than a horse can run. Scouts warn the camp, and families quickly take down tipis and move to the river, or set a back fire to burn a safe zone. Plains peoples knew how to use fire too, burning grass to bring fresh growth for the buffalo.",
  "lakota:thunder": "The Wakíŋyaŋ, Thunder Beings, live in the west. A Lakota (Lakȟóta) who dreams of them becomes a heyókȟa, a sacred clown who does everything backward, laughing when others mourn and shivering in summer. Black Elk (Heȟáka Sápa), whose great vision came when he was nine, saw the Thunder Beings. Thunder is wakȟáŋ, sacred, powerful and dangerous.",
  "lakota:birth": "In 1831, a boy was born on the Grand River and named Jumping Badger; as a young warrior he received his father's name, Tȟatȟáŋka Íyotake, Sitting Bull. Imagined: in a tipi on the plains, a mother gives birth while the grandmothers prepare a beaded turtle amulet for the umbilical cord. Will this child be a leader of the people? They pray to Wakȟáŋ Tȟáŋka.",
  "lakota:death": "On 29 December 1890 at Wounded Knee Creek (Čhaŋkpé Ópi Wakpála), soldiers of the 7th Cavalry opened fire on Big Foot's (Sitȟáŋka) band of Miniconjou (Mnikȟówožu) Lakota (Lakȟóta). Some 250 to 300 men, women and children were killed. The bodies lay frozen in the snow for days. Black Elk (Heȟáka Sápa) said: a people's dream died there. Today descendants hold the Big Foot Memorial Ride each December, a grief kept alive.",

  // ── Americans ──
  "americans:hunt": "In November 1902, President Theodore Roosevelt went bear hunting in Mississippi. His guides tied a black bear to a willow tree for him to shoot, and he refused, calling it unsportsmanlike. A cartoonist drew the scene; a Brooklyn (Breuckelen) shopkeeper made a stuffed bear and asked permission to call it Teddy's bear. Roosevelt also founded national forests and parks to protect what hunters loved.",
  "americans:battle": "Gettysburg, 1 to 3 July 1863. On the third day, some 12,500 Confederates make Pickett's Charge across an open field toward the Union center on Cemetery Ridge. Cannon and rifle fire cut them down; barely half return. Over three days, around 50,000 men are killed, wounded or missing. In November Lincoln spoke for two minutes: that this nation, under God, shall have a new birth of freedom.",
  "americans:gather": "In 1621 the Plymouth settlers, after their first harvest, held a three-day feast with some ninety Wampanoag (Wôpanâak) men and their leader Massasoit (Ousamequin), who brought five deer. Without Wampanoag help, especially Tisquantum, the colonists might not have survived. For many Native people, the fourth Thursday of November is now also a National Day of Mourning, held at Plymouth since 1970.",
  "americans:ritual": "Imagined: on the night before the 1969 Apollo 11 launch, families gather around televisions across America, many bowing their heads in prayer. On Christmas Eve 1968, the Apollo 8 crew, orbiting the Moon, had read from Genesis. Americans hoped for the future and feared the risk. For a country of many faiths, that night was a shared prayer.",
  "americans:rain": "In the 1930s, drought and poor farming turned the southern Plains into the Dust Bowl. On Black Sunday, 14 April 1935, a wall of dust rolled over Oklahoma and Texas, turning day into night. Families waited year after year for rain. Hundreds of thousands left for California. Woody Guthrie sang So Long, It's Been Good to Know Yuh, and Dorothea Lange photographed their faces.",
  "americans:flood": "On 31 May 1889 the South Fork Dam above Johnstown, Pennsylvania, failed after days of rain. A wall of water carrying trees, houses and railroad cars swept into the town, killing 2,209 people. The dam had belonged to a club of wealthy Pittsburgh industrialists. Clara Barton and the young Red Cross arrived and stayed five months, its first great relief effort.",
  "americans:wind": "On 29 August 2005, Hurricane Katrina struck the Gulf Coast. In New Orleans (La Nouvelle-Orléans) the levees failed, and 80 percent of the city flooded. People waited for days on rooftops and in the Superdome for help. More than 1,800 died. Musicians, chefs and families who scattered across the country came back to rebuild, and second-line parades returned to the streets.",
  "americans:fire": "On the night of 8 October 1871 a fire started in or near the O'Leary (Ó Laoghaire) family's barn on DeKoven Street. Driven by strong winds through a city of wooden buildings, the Great Chicago Fire burned for two days, destroying over three square miles and leaving 100,000 homeless. The city rebuilt with stone, steel and ambition; the world's first skyscrapers rose there.",
  "americans:thunder": "In June 1752, the story goes, Benjamin Franklin flew a kite in a thunderstorm in Philadelphia with his son William, a key tied to the string. When the thread fibers stood up and the key sparked, he proved lightning was electricity. He then invented the lightning rod, and thunder over American cities became less frightening, a puzzle science had answered.",
  "americans:birth": "On 12 February 1809, in a one-room log cabin in Kentucky, Nancy Hanks Lincoln gave birth to a son, Abraham. She would die when he was nine. No one in that cabin imagined that he would lead the country through civil war and end slavery. Every American birth carries a little of that hope: anyone might become anything.",
  "americans:death": "In April 1865, after Lincoln's assassination, a funeral train carried his body 1,654 miles from Washington to Springfield, Illinois, retracing his journey to the presidency. Millions stood by the tracks, many at night with bonfires, to watch it pass. In New Orleans (La Nouvelle-Orléans) today, jazz funerals march with a brass band, slow hymns going to the grave and joyful music coming back.",

  // ── Mexicans ──
  "mexicans:hunt": "Imagined: in the Sierra Tarahumara, Rarámuri runners hunt deer the old way, by running them down over hours until the animal tires. On the haciendas of the north, charros rope cattle in the charreada, the national sport born from ranch work. Hunting and herding in Mexico mix Indigenous endurance with the horsemanship brought from Spain.",
  "mexicans:battle": "On 5 May 1862, at Puebla, General Ignacio Zaragoza's poorly equipped Mexican army defeated the French expeditionary force, then reputed the finest army in the world. The French charged uphill against the forts of Loreto and Guadalupe in the rain and were thrown back. France later took Mexico City (Ciudad de México), but Cinco de Mayo became a symbol that Mexico could stand up to empires.",
  "mexicans:gather": "In the milpa, Mexican farmers plant maize, beans and squash together, as their ancestors did for thousands of years; Mexico gave the world maize, chocolate, chili, tomatoes and vanilla. Each region has its own maize, blue, red, black, white. Women grind nixtamal for tortillas at dawn. In the 1940s Norman Borlaug's wheat research in Sonora began the Green Revolution.",
  "mexicans:ritual": "On 12 December, millions of pilgrims arrive at the Basilica of Guadalupe (Basílica de Guadalupe) in Mexico City (Ciudad de México), many on their knees, to honor the Virgin who, the story says, appeared to Juan Diego (Cuauhtlatoatzin) on the hill of Tepeyac (Tepeyacac) in 1531, speaking Nahuatl. Mariachis sing Las Mañanitas at midnight. People ask her for health, work, safe crossing and their children's future.",
  "mexicans:rain": "In 1910 Mexico waited for change under Porfirio Díaz, who had ruled for over thirty years. Francisco Madero called for revolution on 20 November. In the south Emiliano Zapata rose with peasants demanding land: Tierra y Libertad. The waiting broke into a decade of revolution that killed perhaps a million people and remade Mexico.",
  "mexicans:flood": "In the colonial period Mexico City (Ciudad de México), built on a drained lake, flooded again and again. In 1629 a great flood left the city underwater for five years; thousands died, and many moved away. The Spanish dug the Huehuetoca drain (Desagüe de Huehuetoca) to empty the valley, work that continued for centuries. Today the city is still sinking as its aquifers are pumped dry.",
  "mexicans:wind": "In October 2023 Hurricane Otis grew in less than a day from a tropical storm to a Category 5 hurricane, striking Acapulco without warning. Hotels and homes were torn open, boats sunk in the bay. Families waited for help in the ruins. Acapulquenses, proud of their port, began clearing the streets the next morning.",
  "mexicans:fire": "Every year in Mexico, on the night before Easter, Judas effigies made of papier-mâché, packed with fireworks, are burned in the streets. In Tultepec, the fireworks capital, the Feria de la Pirotecnia in March honors San Juan de Dios with toritos, bulls of fireworks, charging through crowds. Fire in Mexico is celebration and danger together.",
  "mexicans:thunder": "On 19 September 1985 at 7:19 in the morning, a magnitude 8.0 earthquake struck Mexico City (Ciudad de México) with a roar like thunder. Buildings collapsed; thousands died. The government was slow, so neighbors organized rescue brigades themselves; the topos dug through rubble by hand. On the same date in 2017, after a drill, another earthquake struck, and the brigades came out again.",
  "mexicans:birth": "Imagined: in a family in Oaxaca, a baby girl is born, and her grandmother wraps her in a rebozo and lights a candle to the Virgin of Guadalupe (Virgen de Guadalupe). Will she be a doctor, an artist like Frida Kahlo, or will she go north for work? The family plans her baptism and, years later, her quinceañera, and hopes.",
  "mexicans:death": "On 1 and 2 November, for Día de Muertos, families build ofrendas with marigolds, candles, sugar skulls, pan de muerto and the favorite foods of the dead. In Pátzcuaro and Mixquic they keep vigil in the cemeteries all night, with music and stories. The marigold petals guide the souls home. Death is not only grief here: it is a reunion.",

  // ── Mapuche ──
  "mapuche:hunt": "Imagined: in the forests of Araucanía, a Mapuche hunter goes after pudú (püdü), the tiny deer, and guanaco (luan) on the slopes, carrying bolas (laki), the weighted throwing cords. Before he enters the forest he asks permission of the ngen, the spirit owners of the woods and waters. In the araucaria forests, families gather piñones (ngülliw), the great pine nuts.",
  "mapuche:battle": "On 25 December 1553, the Mapuche toqui (toki) Lautaro (Leftraru), who had served as Pedro de Valdivia's groom and learned Spanish tactics, led an attack on the fort of Tucapel (Tukapel). Valdivia was captured and killed. The Mapuche resisted the Spanish for over three centuries; the Biobío River became the border. Alonso de Ercilla's epic La Araucana honored their courage.",
  "mapuche:gather": "Imagined: in autumn, Mapuche families climb the Andean slopes to gather piñones (ngülliw) from the araucaria trees, a staple that can be eaten boiled, roasted or ground into flour. The trees, pewen, are sacred, and the Pehuenche (Pewenche), the people of the pewen, are named after them. Children play under the great trees while elders sort the nuts.",
  "mapuche:ritual": "The Nguillatun (Ngillatun) is the great Mapuche community prayer, held to ask Ngenechen (Ngünechen) for good harvests, rain, health and balance. The community gathers around a rewe, a carved post, with offerings. The machi, a spiritual healer, plays the kultrun (kultrung) drum and climbs the rewe in trance. Many families take part in prayer over several days.",
  "mapuche:rain": "Imagined: after years of drought in southern Chile, a Mapuche community near Temuco (Temuko), its rivers diminished by drought and plantations of pine and eucalyptus, gathers for a Nguillatun (Ngillatun) to ask for rain. Elders recall when the rivers ran full. They wait, praying, watching the clouds come from the Pacific.",
  "mapuche:flood": "Mapuche tradition tells of an ancient flood: Kai Kai Vilu (Kaykay Filu), the sea serpent, raised the waters to drown the people, and Treng Treng Vilu (Trengtreng Filu), the serpent of the land, raised the hills so they could climb to safety. On 22 May 1960 the strongest earthquake ever recorded, magnitude 9.5, struck near Valdivia, and a tsunami swept the coast. Many Mapuche saw the old struggle of the serpents once more.",
  "mapuche:wind": "Imagined: a winter storm from the Pacific sweeps over the Araucanía, bending the forests and tearing at the ruka, the thatched houses. Families gather around the central fire, which never goes out, and the elders tell epew, the old stories. The wind is one of the forces that the Mapuche recognize as alive.",
  "mapuche:fire": "Imagined: summer wildfires, fed by drought and pine plantations, sweep through southern Chile. A Mapuche community watches its forest and fields burn and its sacred places threatened. Young people form brigades and go into the smoke to protect homes and the elderly. The fire brings to the surface a long struggle over land.",
  "mapuche:thunder": "Imagined: thunder echoes off the volcanoes Villarrica (Rukapillan) and Llaima, and Mapuche elders say the pillan, the spirits of the volcanoes and ancestors, are speaking. The pillan is honored and feared, associated with thunder, lightning and eruptions. A machi may be consulted to understand what the storm means for the community.",
  "mapuche:birth": "Imagined: a baby is born in a Mapuche ruka in Araucanía, and the grandmother speaks to it first in Mapudungun, the language of the land. The family remembers Lautaro (Leftraru), born around 1534, a boy taken by the Spanish who returned to lead his people. Some hope this child will defend the land; others only pray it grows up healthy and free.",
  "mapuche:death": "When a Mapuche person dies, the community holds an eluwün, a funeral that may last several days. People gather, speak of the dead, and share food. The body is placed in a canoe-shaped coffin, wampo, and carried to the cemetery, and the grave may be marked with a chemamüll, a carved wooden figure. The dead journey west across the sea.",

  // ── Muisca ──
  "muisca:hunt": "Imagined: on the high plateau of Bogotá (Bacatá), Muisca (Muysca) hunters chase white-tailed deer and capture guinea pigs and ducks around the lakes. Deer hunting was restricted, some say reserved for the nobility. The hunters carry spear throwers and nets, and their village chief takes a share. The meat is shared at feasts with chicha (fapqua), the maize beer.",
  "muisca:battle": "In 1537 the Spanish conquistador Gonzalo Jiménez de Quesada climbed into the Muisca (Muysca) highlands from the Magdalena River with his exhausted men. The zipa (psihipqua) Tisquesusa fought, but was killed in 1538. The zaque Quemuenchatocha at Hunza was taken. The Muisca confederation, rich in gold and salt, fell within two years.",
  "muisca:gather": "The Muisca (Muysca) were famous for salt, mined at Zipaquirá and Nemocón by boiling brine in clay pots. Salt cakes were traded across Colombia, with emeralds, cotton cloth and gold. Muisca farmers grew maize, potatoes and quinoa on terraced fields and raised beds. The harvest and the salt trade made them one of the richest peoples of the Andes.",
  "muisca:ritual": "When a new zipa (psihipqua) was made, the Muisca (Muysca) performed a ceremony at Lake Guatavita: the heir, covered in gold dust, was rowed on a raft to the middle of the lake, where he and his companions offered gold and emeralds to the waters. This ceremony inspired the legend of El Dorado. The Muisca Raft, found in 1969, shows it in gold.",
  "muisca:rain": "The Muisca (Muysca) waited for the rains and watched the sky, and their priests, the chyquy, kept a calendar based on moon and sun. Imagined: during a long dry season, the people of Bacatá go in procession to a sacred lake, carrying offerings of tunjos, small gold figures, to ask for rain. The priests fast and watch for signs.",
  "muisca:flood": "In Muisca (Muysca) tradition, the god Chibchacum, angry at the people, flooded the plateau of Bogotá (Bacatá). The people prayed to Bochica, who appeared on a rainbow and struck the rocks with his golden staff, opening a gap for the water to flow out: the Tequendama Falls (Salto del Tequendama). Chibchacum was condemned to carry the world on his shoulders; when he shifts it, the earth trembles.",
  "muisca:wind": "Imagined: cold winds sweep over the páramo, the high moorland above the Muisca (Muysca) villages, carrying mist and rain. Families stay inside their round houses, weaving cotton mantas by the fire. The páramo lakes were sacred, and the wind that came down from them was respected, a messenger of the high places.",
  "muisca:fire": "Sogamoso, called Sugamuxi, held the great Temple of the Sun of the Muisca (Muysca). In 1537, Spanish soldiers looting it by torchlight are said to have set it on fire accidentally; it burned for days. A rebuilt version stands today in the archaeological museum of Sogamoso. For the Muisca it was the loss of their holiest place.",
  "muisca:thunder": "Imagined: thunder rolls over the Bogotá plateau, and the Muisca (Muysca) say it is the gods' voice. Bochica, the culture hero, had taught them weaving and law and given them the rainbow. After a storm, a rainbow, Cuchavira, was taken as a sign of his protection. Women in labor and the sick prayed to Cuchavira.",
  "muisca:birth": "Imagined: in a Muisca (Muysca) village, a woman gives birth and the family makes offerings to Bachué, the mother goddess who rose from Lake Iguaque (Laguna de Iguaque) with a child in her arms and populated the earth. Will this child be a cacique, a priest trained for years in seclusion, or a salt maker? The family offers a small gold tunjo.",
  "muisca:death": "Muisca (Muysca) nobles were mummified, dried and wrapped in fine cotton mantas, and placed in caves or temples with gold and emeralds. Some were carried into battle to give courage. Mourners sang laments for days, and chicha (fapqua) was drunk in their memory. Ordinary people were buried with their tools and pots, ready for the life to come.",

  // ── Tupi ──
  "tupi:hunt": "Tupi hunters along the Brazilian coast moved quietly through the Atlantic forest with bows taller than themselves, hunting tapir, peccary and monkeys. Jean de Léry, a Frenchman who lived among the Tupinambá in 1557, described their skill with the bow and the joy with which they returned to the village, sharing the meat with everyone.",
  "tupi:battle": "In 1554, Hans Staden, a German gunner, was captured by the Tupinambá near São Vicente. He was told he would be eaten in a ritual, the fate of a respected enemy. He wrote that he survived nine months by his wits and prayers until a French ship ransomed him. His book, published in 1557, describes the warfare and rituals of the Tupinambá in vivid detail.",
  "tupi:gather": "Each year Tupi families cut and burned a patch of forest to plant manioc, maize and beans; the ash fertilizes the soil, and after a few years the forest returns. Fire here is ordinary life, not disaster. Women grate the manioc and squeeze out its poison in long woven tubes, the tipiti, to make flour, farinha, still eaten across Brazil.",
  "tupi:ritual": "The Tupi pajé, the shaman, traveled between villages and spoke with the spirits, shaking a gourd rattle, the maracá (mbaraká), while tobacco smoke filled the air. Villagers asked whether the war would go well, whether the harvest would be good. Jesuit missionaries like José de Anchieta wrote in Tupi and tried to replace the pajés; the Tupi language survived as Brazil's lingua franca for centuries.",
  "tupi:rain": "Some Tupi-Guarani peoples migrated in search of Yvy Marã Ey, the Land Without Evil, a place without death or suffering. Prophets, the karaí, led whole villages on long journeys toward the sea or the west, waiting for the world to change. Movements continued into the 20th century among the Guarani. The hope was not for weather, but for a new world.",
  "tupi:flood": "In a Tupinambá myth recorded by the French friar André Thevet in the 1550s, a great flood covered the earth, and only two brothers and their wives survived by climbing a tall palm tree. From them came the Tupinambá and their rivals. The flood was the beginning of the peoples, and of their enmity.",
  "tupi:wind": "Imagined: a summer storm sweeps in from the Atlantic, and the palm-thatched longhouses of a Tupinambá village shake in the wind. Hammocks, a Tupi invention the world borrowed, swing. Families gather around the fires, children cling to their mothers, and the old men say it is the voice of Tupã, the thunder.",
  "tupi:fire": "In September, at the end of the dry season, a Tupi village burns its new field. Men felled the trees months earlier; now the dry wood is set alight, and smoke rises above the forest as the whole village watches. The ash will feed the manioc. Slash-and-burn is ordinary life, the way the forest feeds the people, and after a few years they move on and the forest returns.",
  "tupi:thunder": "Tupã is the voice of the thunder for the Tupi-Guarani peoples. When Jesuit missionaries arrived, they used Tupã as the word for God, and it remains so in the Guarani language today. Imagined: a child in a coastal village hears thunder for the first time and runs to her grandmother, who tells her it is Tupã, passing over the forest.",
  "tupi:birth": "Jean de Léry wrote that when a Tupinambá child was born, the father cut the umbilical cord with his teeth and then lay in his hammock for days, resting and fasting, receiving visitors, as if he had given birth himself, the custom called couvade. The child received a name, sometimes that of an ancestor or a respected enemy.",
  "tupi:death": "Jean de Léry, who lived among the Tupinambá in 1557, saw the women wail for hours over the dead, recounting his deeds: how well he hunted, how bravely he fought. The body was bent into a seated position and buried in a round pit, sometimes inside a large clay urn, with food and belongings. For days afterward food was left, and fires kept, to keep the spirit Aignan (Anhanga) away.",

  // ── Egyptians: siege, triumph, build, voyage, festival, games, drought, quake, plague, wedding ──
  "egyptians:siege": "Megiddo (מגדו), 1457 BCE. The Canaanite princes have shut themselves inside the city, and Thutmose III (𓅝𓄟𓋴) orders his soldiers to cut the orchards and build a wooden wall all the way around it. For seven months no one goes in or out. Inside, the grain jars empty and the children grow thin; outside, Egyptian farmers turned soldiers wait in the heat. At last the princes come out and send their sons as hostages.",
  "egyptians:triumph": "Ahmose (𓇹𓄟𓋴) son of Ebana, a soldier from the town of Elkab (𓈖𓐍𓃀𓊖), carved his life on his tomb wall around 1520 BCE. He fought on the river boats, swam with captives, and seven times received the gold of valor, golden flies hung around his neck before the army. Coming home, he was given fields and servants. His grandson, the scribe Paheri, kept the story so the family would never forget.",
  "egyptians:build": "Around 2560 BCE, an inspector named Merer kept a logbook of his crew of about forty men. The papyri, found at Wadi al-Jarf (وادي الجرف), record their months hauling white limestone by boat from the quarries at Tura across the flooded plain to Giza (الجيزة), for the pyramid of Khufu (𓐍𓅱𓆑𓅱). Gangs called themselves names like Friends of Khufu, ate bread, beer and beef, and pulled the sledges in rhythm to a shouted chant.",
  "egyptians:voyage": "Around 1470 BCE five ships sail for the land of Punt (𓊪𓅱𓈖𓏏𓈉), sent by Hatshepsut (𓄂𓏏𓈙𓊪𓋴𓅱𓏏). They follow the Red Sea south for weeks, until the coast of stilt houses and incense trees appears. The reliefs at Deir el-Bahari (الدير البحري) show the queen of Punt, sacks of myrrh, baboons and ebony carried aboard, and living incense trees in baskets to be planted in Amun's (𓇋𓏠𓈖) garden at Thebes (𓌀𓏏𓊖). Sailors called Punt the land of the god.",
  "egyptians:festival": "When Sopdet (𓋴𓊪𓂧𓏏𓇼), the Dog Star, rises before the sun, the new year begins: Wepet Renpet (𓄋𓊪𓏏𓆳𓏏), the opening of the year. Soon after comes the Festival of Drunkenness for Hathor (𓉡). It recalls how Ra (𓂋𓂝𓇳𓀭) tricked the raging lioness Sekhmet (𓋴𓐍𓅓𓏏) with beer dyed red until she slept and woke as gentle Hathor. People drink, play drums and sistrums through the night, and at dawn are woken to see the goddess.",
  "egyptians:games": "On the walls of the rock tombs at Beni Hasan (بني حسن), painted around 1900 BCE, hundreds of pairs of wrestlers grip, lift and throw each other, every hold shown step by step. Wrestling was training for soldiers and a joy for villagers. In the evenings, people played senet (𓊃𓈖𓏏𓏠), moving pieces across thirty squares; later the game became a journey of the soul, and Tutankhamun (𓇋𓏠𓈖𓏏𓅱𓏏𓋹) took four boards into his tomb.",
  "egyptians:drought": "Around 2150 BCE, when the Nile floods failed again and again, Ankhtifi, governor at Mo'alla (المعلا), wrote on his tomb: all of Upper Egypt was dying of hunger, and people were eating their children. He boasts that he fed his own district and shared grain with his neighbors. Whether or not his boast was true, ordinary farmers remembered those years as the time when the river forgot them.",
  "egyptians:quake": "In 27 BCE an earthquake shook Thebes (𓌀𓏏𓊖) and cracked one of the two giant seated statues of Amenhotep III (𓇋𓏠𓈖𓊵𓏏𓊪). After that, at dawn, the broken stone made a strange sound, perhaps as the dew warmed and dried. Greek and Roman travelers called it Memnon (Μέμνων) greeting his mother, the Dawn, and carved poems on its legs. When the statue was repaired around 200 CE, it fell silent forever.",
  "egyptians:plague": "The mummy of Ramesses V (𓇳𓄟𓋴𓇓), who died around 1145 BCE, still carries pustule scars on its face, perhaps the oldest sign of smallpox known. If a king could die of it, so could the villages. In a time of sickness, people prayed to Sekhmet (𓋴𓐍𓅓𓏏), the lioness who sent plague and could also hold it back, and her wab priests went from house to house as physicians and exorcists.",
  "egyptians:wedding": "Imagined: in a village near Thebes (𓌀𓏏𓊖), the bride's furniture, linen and copper pots are loaded on donkeys and carried through the lanes to her husband's house. Egyptians had no temple wedding; marriage was moving in together, and later a scribe might write a contract listing what the wife brought, which stayed hers if they parted. Neighbors sing and clap, and the couple are wished many children.",

  // ── Babylonians ──
  "babylonians:siege": "Babylon (𒆍𒀭𒊏𒆠), 650 to 648 BCE. The city is besieged by the armies of Ashurbanipal (𒀸𒋩𒆕𒀀) during a war between two royal brothers. Clay sale contracts from those months show the price of barley rising beyond reach. Families sold their children to survive, and letters speak of hunger and sickness in the streets. When the city fell, the king inside died in his burning palace, and the survivors began again.",
  "babylonians:triumph": "Around 1100 BCE, King Nebuchadnezzar I brought the statue of Marduk (𒀭𒀫𒌓) home from Elam, where it had been carried off decades before. Poems describe the god's return: the road from the east, the city gates thrown open, people weeping and singing as Marduk entered his temple Esagila (𒂍𒊕𒅍) again. To Babylonians, the god's absence had meant disorder; his return meant the world was right again.",
  "babylonians:build": "Nebuchadnezzar II wrote that when he rebuilt Etemenanki, the great ziggurat of Marduk (𒀭𒀫𒌓), he carried a basket of earth himself, and had his sons carry bricks of gold and silver. Thousands of workers fired millions of bricks, stamped with the king's name, and laid them in bitumen. Brick makers sang as they pressed clay into molds, and the tower rose above the flat land.",
  "babylonians:voyage": "Herodotus (Ἡρόδοτος) describes round boats of hide stretched over willow frames, floating down the Euphrates from the mountains to Babylon (𒆍𒀭𒊏𒆠). Each carried casks of palm wine and a donkey or two. In Babylon the crews sold their cargo and broke up the boats, selling the wood, then loaded the hides on the donkeys and walked back upstream to build new boats. The river only flows one way.",
  "babylonians:festival": "In Nisannu (𒌚𒁈), the first month of spring, Babylon (𒆍𒀭𒊏𒆠) celebrates Akitu, the new year. For eleven days priests recite the Enuma Elish (𒂊𒉡𒈠𒂊𒇺), the creation story. The high priest strikes the king on the cheek; if tears come, Marduk (𒀭𒀫𒌓) is pleased. Then the gods' statues are carried along the Processional Way through the blue-glazed Ishtar Gate, and crowds line the streets to see them pass.",
  "babylonians:games": "In 177 BCE, a Babylonian scribe named Itti-Marduk-balatu wrote down the rules of the Royal Game of Ur (𒋀𒀊𒆠), already more than two thousand years old. Players threw knucklebones and raced their pieces along twenty squares, with rosette squares bringing luck. The tablet even links squares to omens and fortunes. Boards of this game turn up from Babylon (𒆍𒀭𒊏𒆠) to Crete to India, played in markets and homes.",
  "babylonians:drought": "In the Atrahasis (𒀜𒊏𒄩𒋛) epic, the gods, annoyed by the noise of humanity, send drought: Adad (𒀭𒅎) holds back his rain, the river does not rise, fields turn white with salt, and people grow hungry year after year. Babylonians knew this was not just a story. When the canals ran dry, the granaries of the temple fed the city, and scribes recorded the high price of grain.",
  "babylonians:quake": "Imagined: the ground under Babylon (𒆍𒀭𒊏𒆠) shakes at night, cracking the walls of a mudbrick house in the Merkes quarter. In the morning the family consults the omen series Shumma Alu: if the earth quakes in this month, what follows? Diviners write a report for the king, priests perform a namburbi ritual to undo the bad sign, and the family rebuilds its broken wall.",
  "babylonians:winter": "Babylonian astronomers kept diaries for centuries, noting each night the stars and planets, and also the weather: rain, wind, frost, the price of barley and the level of the river. In some winters they noted severe cold. Imagined: in the month Tebetu, frost lies on the date palms at dawn, and families crowd around clay braziers burning palm fronds.",
  "babylonians:plague": "In the Erra (𒀭𒀴𒊏) epic, the god of plague and war roams Babylonia, killing righteous and wicked alike, until his counselor Ishum (𒀭𒄿𒋳) calms him. Babylonians copied lines of this poem onto small clay amulets and hung them in their houses during epidemics, believing that where the poem was kept, the plague god would pass by. Families waited at home, praying that Erra would spare them.",
  "babylonians:wedding": "In Babylon (𒆍𒀭𒊏𒆠), a groom brings a bride-price, the terhatum, to the bride's father, and the bride comes with her dowry, which remains her own. The families feast, and in some texts the groom pours oil on the bride's head. Hammurabi's (𒄩𒄠𒈬𒊏𒁉) laws protect the wife: if her husband leaves her without cause, he must return her dowry. Imagined: musicians play the lyre as she enters her new house.",

  // ── Assyrians ──
  "assyrians:siege": "Nineveh (𒌷𒉌𒉡𒀀), 612 BCE. The greatest city of the age, with walls twelve kilometers around, is surrounded by Babylonian and Median armies. For three months the defenders hold out, until the Khosr river floods and breaches part of the walls. The city burns. Archaeologists found skeletons in the gates where the last defenders fell. The prophet Nahum (נחום) wrote of the fall, and Assyrians mourned their lost city.",
  "assyrians:triumph": "In 879 BCE Ashurnasirpal II finished his new capital at Kalhu (𒆗𒄷) and held a feast for ten days, recorded on a stone stele: 69,574 guests, including workers, foreign guests and the people of the city. They ate thousands of sheep, cattle and gazelles, and drank thousands of jars of beer and wine. The stele ends: I gave them food and drink, washed them and sent them home in peace and joy.",
  "assyrians:build": "Reliefs from Sennacherib's (𒀭𒌍𒉽𒈨𒌍𒋢) palace at Nineveh (𒌷𒉌𒉡𒀀) show workers hauling a colossal winged bull on a wooden sledge. Hundreds of men pull ropes, others push levers, while overseers shout and a man on top of the bull gives the rhythm. Sennacherib also built a stone aqueduct at Jerwan, bringing mountain water to the city's gardens. His inscription says he made Nineveh splendid like the stars.",
  "assyrians:voyage": "Around 1900 BCE, merchants from the city of Ashur (𒀸𒋩𒆠) led donkey caravans across the mountains to Kanesh (𒅗𒉌𒅖) in Anatolia, carrying tin and woolen cloth. The journey took about six weeks. Thousands of their letters survive: wives in Ashur, who wove the cloth, wrote to husbands abroad, asking for silver, news and come home soon. The trip ended when the walls of Kanesh came into view.",
  "assyrians:festival": "At the city of Ashur (𒀸𒋩𒆠), the new year festival Akitu sends the statue of the god Ashur (𒀭𒀸𒋩) in procession to the Akitu house outside the walls, then back again in triumph. Musicians play harps and drums, and the king wears special robes. Imagined: a family from the countryside comes to the city for the festival, buying bread and figs in the market as the god passes.",
  "assyrians:games": "Guards in the palace of Sargon II at Khorsabad (خورسباد) must have been bored. Scratched into the stone bases of the great winged bulls at the gates are boards for the game of twenty squares, the same one played across the ancient Near East. Imagined: two guards crouch at the gate in the evening, throwing knucklebones and moving their pieces while the bull watches above them.",
  "assyrians:drought": "Imagined: in the dry farming land around Nineveh (𒌷𒉌𒉡𒀀), where barley needs the winter rains, the rains fail for a second year. Officials write to the king: the harvest is poor, the people are moving to the river towns. Royal letters really do report crop failure and locusts, and diviners ask Adad (𒀭𒅎), the storm god, whether he will send rain again.",
  "assyrians:quake": "Assyrian scholars wrote to their king about every sign in heaven and earth. One letter reports an earthquake and explains its meaning from the omen series. Imagined: in Nineveh (𒌷𒉌𒉡𒀀), cracks open in the walls of a family's house, a clay jar falls and breaks, and the next day the priests perform a ritual so the bad omen will pass by the king and the land.",
  "assyrians:winter": "In 714 BCE Sargon II led his army through the Zagros mountains toward Lake Urmia. His long letter to the god Ashur (𒀭𒀸𒋩) describes peaks wrapped in snow and cloud, paths so steep the soldiers climbed on hands and knees. The men cut through forests and carried chariots over passes. For the soldiers it was cold, fear, and the long wait to see the plains below.",
  "assyrians:plague": "The Assyrian eponym chronicle, which named each year after an official, notes plague in the city of Ashur (𒀸𒋩𒆠) in 765 BCE, and again in 759 BCE. The years were also marked by revolt and an eclipse of the sun. For the people of Ashur, these were years of fear: the gods were angry, and families buried their dead while priests performed rites of purification.",
  "assyrians:wedding": "Middle Assyrian laws describe the bridegroom's family bringing gifts to the bride's father, and a feast where the groom pours oil on the bride's head or brings food on a dish. A married woman was veiled in the street. Imagined: in a village near Ashur (𒀸𒋩𒆠), the bride arrives at her new house with her dowry of cloth and jewelry, and neighbors sing and drum.",

  // ── Sumerians ──
  "sumerians:siege": "Ur (𒋀𒀊𒆠), around 2004 BCE. Enemies from Elam (𒉏𒈠𒆠) and the hills surround the city, and the Lament for Ur remembers what followed: no bread, no beer, the people lying in the streets like fish in a dried pond, mothers abandoning children. When the walls fell, the temple of the moon god Nanna (𒀭𒋀𒆠) burned. The goddess Ningal (𒀭𒊩𒌆𒃲) weeps in the poem for her city, and Sumerians wept with her for generations.",
  "sumerians:triumph": "On the Standard of Ur (𒋀𒀊𒆠), made around 2500 BCE, one side shows war and the other shows what came after: a banquet. Seated men raise their cups, servants bring cattle, sheep and fish, and a musician plays a lyre with a bull's head while a singer stands beside him. For the people of Ur, victory meant this: the feast, the music, and the safe return home.",
  "sumerians:build": "Around 2125 BCE, Gudea of Lagash (𒉢𒁓𒆷𒆠) rebuilt the temple Eninnu for the god Ningirsu (𒀭𒊩𒌆𒄈𒋢). His clay cylinders describe it: the city was purified, no harsh word was spoken, mothers did not scold their children. Gudea himself shaped the first brick, and when it came out of the mold well formed, the people rejoiced. Workers carried baskets of earth to the sound of drums.",
  "sumerians:voyage": "Ships from Ur (𒋀𒀊𒆠) sailed down the Gulf to Dilmun (𒉌𒋾𒆠) and Magan (𒈣𒃶𒈾𒆠) to buy copper. A merchant named Ea-nasir, around 1750 BCE, received an angry tablet from a customer named Nanni: you gave me bad copper, and you treated my messenger with contempt. Behind the complaint is a whole sea world: sailors waiting for the wind, the islands of date palms, and the copper mountains of Magan.",
  "sumerians:festival": "Twice a year, Ur (𒋀𒀊𒆠) held the akiti (𒀉𒆠𒋾) festival of the moon god Nanna (𒀭𒋀𒆠). The god's statue sailed in a boat to the akiti house outside the city and returned in procession, while crowds lined the canal banks with offerings. Imagined: children watch the god's boat gliding past, lit with torches, as singers chant the hymns that only temple musicians know.",
  "sumerians:games": "King Shulgi (𒀭𒂄𒄀) of Ur (𒋀𒀊𒆠) boasted, around 2090 BCE, that he ran from Nippur (𒂗𒆤𒆠) to Ur and back in one day, about 160 kilometers, to celebrate festivals in both cities on the same day. Whether true or not, Sumerians loved contests: bronze figures show wrestlers gripping each other's belts, and the royal tombs of Ur held game boards inlaid with shell and lapis.",
  "sumerians:drought": "Around 2020 BCE, as the kingdom of Ur (𒋀𒀊𒆠) weakened, barley grew scarce. Letters to King Ibbi-Sin (𒀭𒄿𒉈𒀭𒂗𒍪) speak of grain priced at sixty times its usual value, and of a governor who could buy grain only far upstream. Fields had also grown white with salt from centuries of irrigation. In the cities, people waited in long lines at the temple granaries for rations of barley.",
  "sumerians:quake": "Imagined: a tremor runs through the city of Nippur (𒂗𒆤𒆠), and cracks appear in the mudbrick walls of the temple Ekur (𒂍𒆳). The priests fear that Enlil (𒀭𒂗𒆤), the god of the wind and the earth, is angry. Bakers and weavers leave their workshops and run into the streets. Afterward, the temple is repaired and a hymn is sung to calm the god's heart.",
  "sumerians:winter": "In the Sumerian poem Summer and Winter, the brothers Emesh and Enten argue over who is more useful. Enten, winter, says he brings the floods, fills the canals, lets fish and birds multiply and the lambs be born. Emesh boasts of the harvest. In the end the god Enlil (𒀭𒂗𒆤) judges for Winter. Imagined: farmers listen to the debate by a fire on a cold night.",
  "sumerians:plague": "Imagined: when fever spreads through the city of Isin (𒉌𒋛𒅔𒆠), families bring small clay dogs to the temple of Ninisina (𒀭𒊩𒌆𒅔𒋛𒈾), goddess of healing. Dogs were her sacred animals, and archaeologists found dog burials around her temple. The asu, the healer, prepares herbs and the ashipu recites incantations. Families pray that the goddess will send her dogs to chase the sickness away.",
  "sumerians:wedding": "Sumerian love songs tell of Inanna (𒀭𒈹) and the shepherd Dumuzi (𒀭𒌉𒍣): the bride bathing, putting on her jewels and waiting at the door as he arrives with gifts of milk and cream. For ordinary families, the groom's family brought gifts and food to the bride's house and the families shared a feast. Imagined: friends sing these old songs as the bride leaves her father's house.",

  // ── Hittites (no flood) ──
  "hittites:siege": "A Hittite tablet tells of the siege of Urshu (𒌷𒌨𒋗) around 1650 BCE. The king sits in his camp while the siege drags on for months: his generals are lazy, the battering ram breaks, the earthen ramp is not finished. The king rages: why have you not fought? For the soldiers, it was waiting, cold and hunger, and the king's anger, until the city finally fell.",
  "hittites:triumph": "Imagined: after the campaign season, the army returns over the mountain passes to Hattusa (𒌷𒄩𒀜𒌅𒊭). The king first goes to the temples to thank the gods who marched beside him, and offerings of bread, beer and sheep are made. The returning soldiers are greeted by their families at the Lion Gate, and the musicians play lyres and drums as the city celebrates.",
  "hittites:build": "At Hattusa (𒌷𒄩𒀜𒌅𒊭) the Hittites built walls of huge stones, gates guarded by stone lions and sphinxes, and a long stone tunnel under the rampart called Yerkapı. Imagined: workers drag blocks up the hill on wooden sledges, singing to keep time, while masons shape the lions' manes and the scribes count the workers' bread rations.",
  "hittites:voyage": "Around 1210 BCE, when hunger spread across Hatti (𒌷𒄩𒀜𒋾), the Hittite king wrote to the king of Ugarit (𐎜𐎂𐎗𐎚) on the coast: send a ship with grain, two thousand measures, to the port of Ura. It is a matter of life and death. Imagined: in a village on the plateau, people wait for weeks for news of the ships, for the carts of grain crossing the mountains.",
  "hittites:festival": "Purulli (𒁍𒊒𒌌𒇷), the spring festival at the holy city of Nerik (𒌷𒉈𒊑𒅅), welcomed the new year. The priests recited the story of the storm god's fight with the serpent Illuyanka (𒈲𒅋𒇻𒅀𒀭𒅗): the serpent defeats him, and the goddess Inara (𒀭𒄿𒈾𒊏) gets the serpent drunk at a feast so the storm god can kill it. The land is renewed, and the festival celebrates the return of fertility.",
  "hittites:games": "Hittite festival texts describe contests: foot races, horse races, wrestling and stone throwing. In one festival, young men run a race, and the winner receives a cloak from the king. Imagined: villagers gather in the meadow outside Hattusa (𒌷𒄩𒀜𒌅𒊭) to watch, cheering their favorites, and the winner wears the cloak home with pride.",
  "hittites:drought": "A Hittite myth tells of Telipinu (𒀭𒋼𒇷𒉿𒉡), the god of farming, who leaves in anger. Fog covers the windows, the hearth fire chokes, sheep reject their lambs, the grain does not grow and gods and men are starving. The gods search everywhere; a bee finally finds him asleep. The myth was recited to call back fertility in times of drought and famine.",
  "hittites:quake": "Anatolia has always been a land of earthquakes. Imagined: in a town in the Hittite heartland, the walls of the houses shake and some fall. The priests consult oracles: which god is angry, and why? They offer bread and wine, read the signs in the liver of a sheep, and the people rebuild their houses with stone foundations and timber frames.",
  "hittites:winter": "On the high plateau of central Anatolia, winters are long and snowy. Hittite kings stopped campaigning and returned to Hattusa (𒌷𒄩𒀜𒌅𒊭) for the winter festivals. Imagined: snow covers the roofs of the city, families gather around their hearths, burning wood and dried dung, and tell stories while the oxen are kept warm in the stables.",
  "hittites:plague": "Around 1320 BCE, King Mursili II (𒈬𒌨𒅆𒇷) prayed to the gods: the plague has raged in Hatti (𒌷𒄩𒀜𒋾) for twenty years. It came with prisoners from a war. His prayers, recorded on clay tablets, beg the gods to stop the dying. He asks what sin caused it, confessing his father's broken oaths. In every village, people prayed with him and waited for the sickness to end.",
  "hittites:wedding": "Hittite laws mention the kusata, the bride-price paid by the groom's family to the bride's family. After the bride-price was paid, a feast was held and the bride moved to her husband's house. Imagined: in a village near Hattusa (𒌷𒄩𒀜𒌅𒊭), the bride's family prepares bread and beer, and singers sing as she is led to her new home.",

  // ── Phoenicians ──
  "phoenicians:siege": "Tyre (𐤑𐤓), 332 BCE. The island city refuses to open its gates to Alexander (Ἀλέξανδρος), who builds a stone causeway across the sea to reach it. For seven months the Tyrians fight back, sending fire ships to burn the siege towers and divers to cut the anchor ropes. When the walls finally fell, thousands died and many were sold into slavery. The causeway still joins Tyre to the land.",
  "phoenicians:triumph": "Imagined: the ships return to Sidon (𐤑𐤃𐤍) after a hard sea fight, their sails patched, and the whole harbor runs to the quay. Families search for faces at the oars. The captains offer the first share of the spoils to Eshmun (𐤀𐤔𐤌𐤍) and Astarte (𐤏𐤔𐤕𐤓𐤕), and that night the city feasts, with music of lyres and double pipes rising from the rooftops.",
  "phoenicians:build": "Hiram (𐤇𐤓𐤌) of Tyre (𐤑𐤓) sent cedar wood and skilled craftsmen to build the temple in Jerusalem (ירושלים), according to the Bible. Workers felled cedars in the mountains of Lebanon, dragged them down to the sea, tied them into rafts and floated them along the coast. Imagined: the woodcutters sing as the great trunks fall and roll down toward the sea.",
  "phoenicians:voyage": "Around 600 BCE, according to Herodotus (Ἡρόδοτος), Phoenician sailors set out from the Red Sea for Pharaoh Necho II to sail around Africa. Each autumn they landed, sowed grain, waited for the harvest, then sailed on. After three years they came back through the Pillars of Heracles (Ἡρακλῆς). They reported the sun on their right as they sailed west, which Herodotus doubted, and which proves they did it.",
  "phoenicians:festival": "Every spring Tyre (𐤑𐤓) celebrated the egersis (ἔγερσις), the awakening of Melqart (𐤌𐤋𐤒𐤓𐤕), lord of the city. The god was said to die and rise again; an effigy was burned, and then he was awakened. Imagined: the people of Tyre crowd the temple court with offerings of incense and quail, and musicians play as the priests announce that Melqart lives again.",
  "phoenicians:games": "In Hellenistic times, Tyre (𐤑𐤓) held games every five years in honor of Heracles-Melqart (𐤌𐤋𐤒𐤓𐤕), and cities sent delegations, as the second book of Maccabees records. Imagined: young Tyrians race on the beach and wrestle in the sand, sailors bet on boat races in the harbor, and the winners carry palm branches through the crowd.",
  "phoenicians:drought": "According to Menander (Μένανδρος) of Ephesus (Ἔφεσος), quoted by Josephus (Ἰώσηπος), during the reign of Ithobaal (𐤀𐤕𐤁𐤏𐤋) of Tyre (𐤑𐤓) there was a great drought, and no rain fell for a whole year until the king made prayers and thunder came. Imagined: cisterns run dry, the people of Tyre wait for water ships from the mainland, and women pray at the temple of Astarte (𐤏𐤔𐤕𐤓𐤕).",
  "phoenicians:quake": "Imagined: in Byblos (𐤂𐤁𐤋), a strong earthquake cracks the walls of the old temple, and the sea draws back from the harbor, then returns in a wave over the ships. Fishermen run to higher ground. Afterward the people make offerings to Baalat Gebal (𐤁𐤏𐤋𐤕 𐤂𐤁𐤋), the lady of the city, and rebuild the walls with great blocks of stone.",
  "phoenicians:winter": "In winter the sea was closed: storms made sailing dangerous, and Phoenician ships were hauled ashore at Sidon (𐤑𐤃𐤍) and Tyre (𐤑𐤓). Imagined: sailors spend the winter mending sails and ropes, caulking hulls with pitch, telling stories of distant ports, and waiting for spring when the sea opens again and the ships set out for Cyprus and Carthage (𐤒𐤓𐤕𐤇𐤃𐤔𐤕).",
  "phoenicians:plague": "Near Sidon (𐤑𐤃𐤍) stands the temple of Eshmun (𐤀𐤔𐤌𐤍), the healing god, with pools fed by a sacred spring. The sick came to wash, and parents left stone statues of their children as thanks for healing. Imagined: during an epidemic, families crowd the temple's pools and pray to Eshmun, while the priests sing and offer incense.",
  "phoenicians:wedding": "Imagined: in Tyre (𐤑𐤓) the groom's family brings gifts of purple cloth and silver to the bride's house. The bride, adorned with gold earrings and necklaces of glass beads, is led to her new home with music. Phoenician women wore elaborate jewelry, and the couple asks Astarte (𐤏𐤔𐤕𐤓𐤕) and Baal (𐤁𐤏𐤋) for many children and a safe life by the sea.",

  // ── Palmyrans (no flood, no winter) ──
  "palmyrans:siege": "In 272 CE, Aurelian's (Aurelianus) army surrounded Palmyra (𐡶𐡣𐡬𐡴). Food grew short, and the queen Zenobia (𐡡𐡶𐡦𐡡𐡩) slipped out on a fast camel toward the Euphrates (Εὐφράτης), hoping to bring help, but she was caught at the river. The city opened its gates. For the people of Palmyra, those weeks meant fear, hunger and waiting to learn what the emperor would do with them.",
  "palmyrans:triumph": "Imagined: the caravan guards return from a successful fight against desert raiders, their camels loaded with recovered goods. The merchants thank them with a statue in the colonnade and an inscription honoring their leader. That evening the city feasts, with musicians and dancers, and families gather in the banquet halls to celebrate their safety.",
  "palmyrans:build": "In 32 CE, the people of Palmyra (𐡶𐡣𐡬𐡴) dedicated the great temple of Bel (𐡡𐡫), with columns of golden limestone. Builders cut the stone from nearby quarries, and stone carvers shaped capitals and friezes showing camels, gods and offerings. Imagined: workers sing as they lift blocks with wooden cranes, and children watch from the shade.",
  "palmyrans:voyage": "Palmyrene caravans crossed the desert to the Euphrates (Εὐφράτης), then sailed down the river to Charax (Χάραξ) on the Gulf, and some went on by ship to India. Inscriptions honor caravan leaders like Soados who protected the merchants. Imagined: after weeks in the desert, the caravan sees the palm groves of the oasis, and the city comes out to meet them.",
  "palmyrans:festival": "In the month of Nisan (𐡮𐡩𐡯𐡭), Palmyra (𐡶𐡣𐡬𐡴) celebrated the festival of Bel (𐡡𐡫) with processions and sacrifices. The people met in banquet groups, the marzeah (𐡬𐡴𐡦𐡧𐡠), with small clay tokens as tickets to the feast. Imagined: guests recline on couches in the temple court, eating and drinking, while musicians play and the priests offer incense.",
  "palmyrans:games": "Imagined: on a festival day, the young men of Palmyra (𐡶𐡣𐡬𐡴) race their horses and camels on the plain outside the city, where the caravans gather. Palmyrene archers and riders were famous, and the crowds cheer from the walls. The winner receives a crown of palm leaves and his family invites the neighbors to a feast.",
  "palmyrans:drought": "Palmyra's (𐡶𐡣𐡬𐡴) life depended on the Efqa (𐡠𐡱𐡳𐡠) spring, guarded by the god Yarhibol (𐡩𐡴𐡧𐡡𐡥𐡫). Imagined: in a dry year, the spring runs low and the gardens wither. The people offer prayers and incense at the spring, the caravans bring grain from the Euphrates (Εὐφράτης), and families ration their water and wait for winter rains.",
  "palmyrans:quake": "Imagined: an earthquake shakes Palmyra (𐡶𐡣𐡬𐡴), and some columns of the colonnade fall into the street. The people rush out of their houses, frightened. Afterward the stone carvers set to work again, raising the fallen columns and repairing the temples, while the priests offer sacrifices to calm the gods.",
  "palmyrans:plague": "Around 165 CE, a great epidemic, the Antonine plague, spread across the Roman east. Imagined: in Palmyra (𐡶𐡣𐡬𐡴), the sickness arrives with the caravans, and families bury their dead in the tower tombs outside the city, with carved portraits of the dead looking out. Those who survive pray to Bel (𐡡𐡫) and Yarhibol (𐡩𐡴𐡧𐡡𐡥𐡫) for the city's health.",
  "palmyrans:wedding": "Imagined: a Palmyrene bride, wearing gold earrings, necklaces and a veil like the women in the city's funerary portraits, is led to her husband's house. The families sign a contract and hold a feast. Musicians play and the guests sing as the couple is wished long life and many children.",

  // ── Minoans ──
  "minoans:siege": "Imagined: Knossos (𐀒𐀜𐀰), around 1450 BCE. The palace has no great walls; the sea was always its defense. Now ships no one recognizes are on the horizon and fires burn along the coast. Families carry their oil jars and children up into the hills, while the old storerooms of the palace, full of giant pithoi, are left behind. When they return, the palace has burned.",
  "minoans:triumph": "The Flotilla Fresco from Akrotiri (Ακρωτήρι) on Thera (Θήρα) shows a fleet arriving at a harbor town. People lean from windows and rooftops to watch, dolphins leap beside the ships, and the boats are decorated with garlands. Whether returning from a voyage or a victory, it is a scene of homecoming. Imagined: mothers run down to the quay, and the island celebrates the safe return.",
  "minoans:build": "At Knossos (𐀒𐀜𐀰), archaeologists found marks carved into the stone blocks: double axes, stars and tridents, perhaps the signs of different work gangs. The palace had light wells, staircases, drains and painted walls. Imagined: masons cut gypsum from the hills, carpenters fit wooden columns that narrow at the bottom, and workers sing as they haul the stones into place.",
  "minoans:voyage": "In the tomb of the Egyptian vizier Rekhmire at Thebes, men from Keftiu, probably Crete (Κρήτη), carry gifts: tall rhyta, copper ingots and decorated vases. They had sailed across open sea, five days or more with a north wind, to Egypt. Imagined: a young sailor sees the Nile delta for the first time and wonders at the land without mountains.",
  "minoans:festival": "On the Harvester Vase from Hagia Triada (Αγία Τριάδα), around 1500 BCE, a crowd of men march with farming tools on their shoulders, mouths open in song. One shakes a sistrum, and some seem to laugh. It is one of the most joyful scenes in ancient art: a harvest festival, a procession of singing farmers, celebrating the end of the hard work.",
  "minoans:games": "The Bull-Leaping Fresco at Knossos (𐀒𐀜𐀰) shows a youth somersaulting over the back of a charging bull while two others, one grasping its horns, help. Seal stones and bronze figures show the same daring leap. Whether sport or sacred rite, it was the great contest of the island, watched by crowds who held their breath as the bull lowered its head.",
  "minoans:drought": "Imagined: a dry summer follows a dry winter, and the springs of the Mesara (Μεσαρά) plain run low. The palace storerooms with their giant pithoi of grain and oil become the island's hope. Officials record the rations on clay tablets in Linear A, and people climb to the peak sanctuaries, leaving small clay figures and praying for rain.",
  "minoans:quake": "Around 1600 BCE the volcano of Thera (Θήρα) exploded in one of the greatest eruptions in human history. At Akrotiri (Ακρωτήρι), earthquakes had already warned the people, and they left: no bodies have been found in the buried town, only beds, pots and painted walls under the ash. Waves and ash reached Crete (Κρήτη), and the island's world was never quite the same.",
  "minoans:winter": "Imagined: snow lies on the peaks of Mount Ida (Ἴδη) and the White Mountains (Λευκά Όρη), and storms close the sea. The ships are pulled up on the beaches, and the sailors stay home. In the villages, families gather by the hearth, weaving wool and pressing the last olives, telling stories of the bull and the mountain goddess.",
  "minoans:plague": "An Egyptian medical papyrus, now in London, contains a spell against the Asiatic disease written in the language of Keftiu, the Egyptian name for Crete (Κρήτη). It is among the very few words of the Minoan language we have. Imagined: in a Cretan town, a healer chants this spell over a sick child, while the mother waits by the door.",
  "minoans:wedding": "Imagined: a Minoan bride in a flounced skirt and jewels of gold is led to her husband's house, with women dancing in a circle and a lyre player at the front. Frescoes show women in festive dress, dancing in olive groves. The families feast on grapes, olives, fish and honey, and toast the couple with wine.",

  // ── Greeks ──
  "greeks:siege": "Plataea (Πλάταια), 429 to 427 BCE. The small city is surrounded by a double wall built by Spartans (Σπαρτιᾶται) and Thebans (Θηβαῖοι). On a stormy winter night, about two hundred Plataeans (Πλαταιεῖς) escape over the siege wall with ladders, each wearing a shoe only on the left foot for grip in the mud. The rest hold out until hunger forces surrender. Thucydides (Θουκυδίδης) tells the story with sorrow.",
  "greeks:triumph": "After the sea battle at Salamis (Σαλαμίς) in 480 BCE, a victory celebration was held, and the young Sophocles (Σοφοκλῆς), still a boy, is said to have led the chorus of youths singing the paean, naked and anointed with oil, with a lyre in his hand. For the Greeks (Ἕλληνες) it was joy and relief: homes, temples and families had survived.",
  "greeks:build": "Around 530 BCE, the engineer Eupalinos (Εὐπαλῖνος) of Megara (Μέγαρα) dug a tunnel through a mountain on Samos (Σάμος) to bring water to the city. Two teams started from opposite sides and met in the middle, more than a kilometer long, only a little off. Herodotus (Ἡρόδοτος) called it one of the greatest works of the Greeks (Ἕλληνες). Imagined: when the two teams heard each other's hammers, they cheered.",
  "greeks:voyage": "Around 631 BCE, the people of Thera (Θήρα), suffering from drought, were told by the oracle at Delphi (Δελφοί) to found a city in Libya (Λιβύη). Two ships of young men drew lots and sailed south. At first they failed and tried to return home, but were driven back by stones. They tried again and found a spring in the hills, and there founded Cyrene (Κυρήνη).",
  "greeks:festival": "At the Anthesteria (Ἀνθεστήρια), the spring festival of Dionysus (Διόνυσος), the jars of new wine were opened. Children of three years old were crowned with flowers and given little jugs, choes, decorated with pictures of children playing. Adults held drinking contests. On the last day, the spirits of the dead were thought to walk, and doors were smeared with pitch.",
  "greeks:games": "Olympia (Ὀλυμπία), 448 BCE. Diagoras (Διαγόρας) of Rhodes (Ῥόδος), himself a great Olympic boxer, watches his two sons win their contests. They lift him on their shoulders and carry him through the crowd, and a Spartan (Σπαρτιάτης) calls out: die now, Diagoras, for you will never climb to Olympus (Ὄλυμπος). The games were for honor only: the prize was a crown of wild olive.",
  "greeks:drought": "On Aegina (Αἴγινα), people told how a great drought struck all of Greece, and the oracle said only Aeacus (Αἰακός), the most pious of men, could end it. He went up the mountain and prayed to Zeus (Ζεύς), and rain fell. A sanctuary of Zeus stood on that peak. Imagined: in dry years, farmers climb the mountain with offerings, watching for clouds over the summit.",
  "greeks:quake": "One winter night in 373 BCE, an earthquake struck Helike (Ἑλίκη) on the Gulf of Corinth (Κόρινθος), and the sea swept over the city. Not one person survived. For centuries afterward, travelers said they could see the ruins under the water, and the bronze statue of Poseidon (Ποσειδῶν) still standing on the seabed, catching fishermen's nets.",
  "greeks:winter": "Hesiod (Ἡσίοδος), the farmer poet, warns of the month of Lenaion (Ληναιών): bad days that skin the oxen, when the north wind blows from Thrace (Θρᾴκη) and roars over the sea. The boneless one gnaws his foot in his fireless home. Stay indoors, he says, wear a soft cloak and a long tunic, and make your sandals of oxhide lined with felt.",
  "greeks:plague": "Pausanias (Παυσανίας) tells that the people of Phigalia (Φιγάλεια) in Arcadia (Ἀρκαδία) built a temple at Bassae (Βάσσαι) to Apollo Epikourios (Ἀπόλλων Ἐπικούριος), Apollo the helper, because he had saved them from a plague. The temple still stands on the mountain. Imagined: during the sickness, families climb the slopes with offerings, singing hymns to Apollo as the clouds pass below them.",
  "greeks:wedding": "On the wedding day the Greek bride bathes in water carried from a sacred spring in a tall jar, the loutrophoros. After the feast, the groom takes her by the wrist and leads her to his house in a cart, by torchlight, as friends sing the hymenaios. At the door she eats a quince or a date, and the guests throw nuts and dried fruit.",

  // ── Athenians ──
  "athenians:siege": "Athens (Ἀθῆναι), 405 to 404 BCE. After the fleet was lost at Aegospotami (Αἰγὸς ποταμοί), Spartan ships closed the Piraeus (Πειραιεύς), and Athens was besieged by land and sea. Xenophon (Ξενοφῶν) describes the people starving, many dying of hunger in the streets. When the city surrendered, the Long Walls (Μακρὰ Τείχη) were torn down to the music of flute girls, while the victors said that freedom had begun for Greece (Ἑλλάς).",
  "athenians:triumph": "Marathon (Μαραθών), 490 BCE. The Athenians (Ἀθηναῖοι) defeated the Persian army on the plain, then marched back to Athens (Ἀθῆναι) the same day to protect the city. The legend says a runner arrived in the agora, said rejoice, we win, and died. The 192 Athenian dead were buried in a mound on the battlefield, which still stands. Aeschylus (Αἰσχύλος) wanted his gravestone to say only that he fought there.",
  "athenians:build": "Plutarch (Πλούταρχος) describes the trades that built the Parthenon (Παρθενών): carpenters, sculptors, coppersmiths, stonecutters, dyers, goldsmiths, ivory workers, painters and road builders. Inscriptions from the Erechtheion (Ἐρέχθειον) list workers, citizens, foreigners and slaves alike, each paid a drachma a day. Imagined: the sound of chisels on Pentelic marble rings out across the city.",
  "athenians:voyage": "In 415 BCE the fleet sailed for Sicily (Σικελία). Thucydides (Θουκυδίδης) describes the whole city coming down to the Piraeus (Πειραιεύς) at dawn. A trumpet called for silence, and the paean was sung while the sailors poured libations from gold and silver cups. Then the ships raced each other to Aegina (Αἴγινα). Few of them would return; the hope of that morning stayed in memory.",
  "athenians:festival": "At the Great Panathenaia (Παναθήναια τὰ μεγάλα), every four years, a new robe, the peplos, woven by young women of Athens (Ἀθῆναι), was carried to the statue of Athena (Ἀθηνᾶ) on the Acropolis (Ἀκρόπολις). The procession included riders, chariots, elders with olive branches, and girls carrying baskets. The Parthenon (Παρθενών) frieze shows this procession, and the whole city took part.",
  "athenians:games": "At the Panathenaic games, the prize for winners was olive oil from Athena's (Ἀθηνᾶ) sacred trees, given in large painted amphorae showing the goddess on one side and the contest on the other. A chariot winner could receive 140 amphorae. There was also a torch race at night, where teams ran relays carrying a flame to the altar.",
  "athenians:drought": "Between 330 and 326 BCE, a grain shortage struck Athens (Ἀθῆναι) and much of Greece (Ἑλλάς). A stone inscription from Cyrene (Κυρήνη) lists the cities it sent grain to, with Athens at the top. Athenian merchants who brought grain cheaply were honored with crowns. Imagined: families wait in line at the grain market of Piraeus (Πειραιεύς), watching the horizon for ships.",
  "athenians:quake": "Just before the Peloponnesian War (Πελοποννησιακὸς πόλεμος) began, in 431 BCE, the sacred island of Delos (Δῆλος) was shaken by an earthquake, for the first time in memory, Thucydides (Θουκυδίδης) wrote. Athenians (Ἀθηναῖοι), who looked after the sanctuary of Apollo (Ἀπόλλων) there, took it as a sign of coming disaster. Imagined: priests and pilgrims gather on the shaken island, unsure what the god is saying.",
  "athenians:winter": "In the winter siege of Potidaea (Ποτείδαια), 432 BCE, the cold was terrible. Soldiers wrapped their feet in felt and fleece. Alcibiades (Ἀλκιβιάδης) later told how Socrates (Σωκράτης) walked barefoot on the ice in his ordinary cloak, more easily than the others in their shoes. The soldiers did not know whether to admire him or resent him.",
  "athenians:plague": "In 430 BCE, plague broke out in Athens (Ἀθῆναι) while country people crowded inside the walls. Thucydides (Θουκυδίδης), who caught the disease himself and survived, describes fever, thirst and the dead lying in temples. People stopped caring about laws or gods. Pericles (Περικλῆς) died of it. It is one of the most vivid accounts of an epidemic in history.",
  "athenians:wedding": "Athenians (Ἀθηναῖοι) preferred to marry in the month of Gamelion (Γαμηλιών), sacred to Hera (Ἥρα). On the eve, the bride dedicates her toys and a lock of hair to Artemis (Ἄρτεμις). After the feast, the procession by torchlight takes her to her new home, the bride's mother carrying the torches. On the next day, the families bring wedding gifts, the epaulia.",

  // ── Spartans ──
  "spartans:siege": "Sphacteria (Σφακτηρία), 425 BCE. Four hundred and twenty Spartans (Σπαρτιᾶται) are trapped on a small island, surrounded by the Athenian fleet. Helots swim at night with bags of poppy seed mixed with honey and linseed to feed them. After seventy-two days, attacked from all sides, 292 survivors surrender. All of Greece (Ἑλλάς) was shocked: Spartans had never been expected to surrender.",
  "spartans:triumph": "Plutarch (Πλούταρχος) says that when Spartans (Σπαρτιᾶται) won a victory by cunning, they sacrificed an ox to Ares (Ἄρης), but for a victory in open battle, only a rooster: they thought winning in battle was simply their duty. Imagined: after the battle, the soldiers return home, and the women and old men welcome them, but without loud celebration.",
  "spartans:build": "Spartan law required that houses be built with only an axe for the roof and a saw for the doors, so no one would want luxury, Plutarch (Πλούταρχος) writes. For centuries Sparta (Σπάρτη) had no walls; it was said that the wall of Sparta was its men. Imagined: neighbors help each other raise a simple house, with wooden beams and earthen walls.",
  "spartans:voyage": "Around 706 BCE, a group of young Spartans (Σπαρτιᾶται) called the Partheniai (Παρθενίαι), born during a long war and denied full citizenship, sailed west to found the city of Taras (Τάρας) in southern Italy. The oracle told them to settle where they saw rain from a clear sky. Their leader's wife wept on his head as he slept, and he knew: this was the place.",
  "spartans:festival": "The Karneia (Κάρνεια), the festival of Apollo (Ἀπόλλων), was so sacred to Spartans (Σπαρτιᾶται) that they would not march out to war until it ended. That is why they arrived late at Marathon (Μαραθών). During the festival, young men chased a runner wearing garlands; if they caught him, it was good luck for the city. Tents were set up and meals shared.",
  "spartans:games": "In 396 BCE, Kyniska (Κυνίσκα), daughter of a Spartan king, became the first woman to win at Olympia (Ὀλυμπία), with her four-horse chariot. Women could not compete themselves, but owners won the prize. Her inscription reads: I alone of all women in Greece (Ἑλλάς) won this crown. Spartan girls also raced and wrestled, unusual in Greece.",
  "spartans:drought": "Imagined: in a dry summer, the Eurotas (Εὐρώτας) river shrinks to a trickle, and the helots (εἵλωτες) who work the fields of Laconia (Λακωνική) struggle to bring in the grain. The common messes, the syssitia, must still be supplied. Families pray to Zeus (Ζεύς) on Mount Taygetos (Ταΰγετος), and the elders send to Delphi (Δελφοί) to ask what the gods want.",
  "spartans:quake": "In 464 BCE, a massive earthquake destroyed Sparta (Σπάρτη). Plutarch (Πλούταρχος) says only five houses stood. Young men exercising in the gymnasium were killed when it collapsed. King Archidamus (Ἀρχίδαμος) called the citizens to arms, and the helots (εἵλωτες) who rose up found the Spartans (Σπαρτιᾶται) ready. The disaster weakened Sparta for a generation.",
  "spartans:winter": "Plutarch (Πλούταρχος) describes Spartan boys in training: one cloak a year, no shoes, sleeping on beds of reeds they pulled by hand from the Eurotas (Εὐρώτας) river, without a knife. In winter they mixed in thistledown for warmth. Imagined: boys shiver on their reed beds in a cold barracks, hungry, learning to endure.",
  "spartans:plague": "Imagined: when fever spreads among the soldiers in a camp, the Spartans (Σπαρτιᾶται) pray to Apollo (Ἀπόλλων) and to Asclepius (Ἀσκληπιός), and the sick are tended by their comrades. Spartans prided themselves on enduring pain without complaint. In the city, mothers wait for news, ready to hear the worst without weeping in public.",
  "spartans:wedding": "Plutarch (Πλούταρχος) describes the Spartan wedding: the bride's hair is cut short, she wears a man's cloak and sandals, and lies alone on a pallet in the dark. The groom comes from his mess, spends a short time with her, then returns to sleep with his comrades. For a long time, couples met only in secret, sometimes having children before seeing each other by day.",

  // ── Macedonians ──
  "macedonians:siege": "Pydna (Πύδνα), 316 BCE. Olympias (Ὀλυμπιάς), mother of Alexander (Ἀλέξανδρος), is shut inside the town with her household and soldiers, besieged through the winter by Cassander (Κάσσανδρος). Food runs out: the soldiers eat their horses, the elephants are fed sawdust and die, and people die of hunger in the streets. In spring, the town surrenders. Diodorus (Διόδωρος) tells the story.",
  "macedonians:triumph": "In 324 BCE, Craterus (Κρατερός) led about ten thousand veterans of Alexander's (Ἀλέξανδρος) army home to Macedonia (Μακεδονία). They had marched from Pella (Πέλλα) to India and back, more than ten years. Imagined: in the villages, mothers and wives who had waited for a decade search the faces of grey-haired men, hearing stories of Persia, elephants and the end of the world.",
  "macedonians:build": "When Alexander (Ἀλέξανδρος) founded Alexandria (Ἀλεξάνδρεια) in 331 BCE, the architect Deinocrates (Δεινοκράτης) had no chalk to mark the city plan, so they used barley meal. Flocks of birds flew down and ate it. The diviners said this meant the city would feed many peoples. Imagined: workers dig the first trenches, laughing at the birds, and the city's streets take shape.",
  "macedonians:voyage": "In 325 BCE, Nearchus (Νέαρχος) led a fleet from the mouth of the Indus along the barren coast toward the Persian Gulf. They met whales, the fish-eaters of the shore, and hunger. When Nearchus finally reached Alexander (Ἀλέξανδρος), he and his men were so thin and burned that the king did not recognize them, and wept to see them alive.",
  "macedonians:festival": "In spring, the Macedonian army celebrated the Xandika (Ξανδικά), a purification festival. The army marched between the two halves of a sacrificed dog, then held mock battles and races. Imagined: the soldiers laugh and shout as they charge each other with wooden weapons, the start of a new campaign season.",
  "macedonians:games": "Plutarch (Πλούταρχος) says that on the day Alexander (Ἀλέξανδρος) was born in 356 BCE, Philip (Φίλιππος) received three pieces of good news: a general had won a battle, his horse had won at the Olympic games, and his son was born. Imagined: in Pella (Πέλλα), the young men race horses on the plain, imitating the Olympic champion.",
  "macedonians:drought": "Imagined: in a dry year, the Macedonian plains around Pella (Πέλλα) and Aigai (Αἰγαί) turn brown, and the shepherds drive their flocks up to the mountain pastures early. Farmers pray to Zeus (Ζεύς) and Demeter (Δημήτηρ), and the kings send grain from the royal storehouses, while families make do with less bread.",
  "macedonians:quake": "Imagined: an earthquake shakes Aigai (Αἰγαί), and the walls of houses crack. Shepherds in the hills see rockfalls and frightened flocks. Afterward the people make offerings to Poseidon (Ποσειδῶν), the earth-shaker, and rebuild their homes with stone and timber, helping one another lift the heavy beams back into place.",
  "macedonians:winter": "In 329 BCE, Alexander's (Ἀλέξανδρος) army crossed the Hindu Kush in snow. The soldiers ran out of food and ate their pack animals raw, without wood for fires. Some went snow-blind, others froze. Imagined: a Macedonian soldier, far from home, warms his hands at a tiny fire and thinks of the hearth in his village.",
  "macedonians:plague": "Imagined: fever spreads through the army camp in Babylon in the hot season, and soldiers lie sick in their tents. The doctors give herbs and wine, and the men pray to Asclepius (Ἀσκληπιός). Families at home in Macedonia (Μακεδονία) wait months for news, not knowing who has survived.",
  "macedonians:wedding": "Hippolochus (Ἱππόλοχος) described the wedding feast of a Macedonian named Caranus (Κάρανος): twenty guests were given silver cups, gold crowns and platters of food, and flute girls and dancers entertained them. Imagined: at a village wedding, the families share a feast of lamb and wine, and guests dance late into the night.",

  // ── Romans ──
  "romans:siege": "Around 390 BCE the Gauls take Rome (Roma), and the last defenders hold only the Capitoline (Capitolium) hill. For months they are hungry, watching their city burn below. One night the Gauls climb the cliff in silence, but the sacred geese of Juno (Iuno), spared despite the hunger, begin to honk and flap. Marcus Manlius wakes and throws the first attacker down. Romans (Romani) remembered the geese for centuries.",
  "romans:triumph": "A Roman triumph: the general rides through the city in a four-horse chariot, his face painted red, wearing the purple toga of Jupiter (Iuppiter). Prisoners, captured treasure and painted pictures of the battles go before him. Behind him march his soldiers, singing rude songs about their own commander, as Caesar's men did, to keep him humble. The procession ends at the temple of Jupiter.",
  "romans:build": "Hadrian's (Hadrianus) Wall, begun in 122 CE, was built by legionaries, not slaves. Along the wall, small inscribed stones record which century of soldiers built each stretch: the century of so-and-so built this. Imagined: soldiers far from home cut and lay stones in the rain, joking and complaining, while their centurion measures the work, and the wall stretches across the hills.",
  "romans:voyage": "Lucian (Λουκιανός) describes the grain ship Isis, blown off course to the Piraeus (Πειραιεύς) around 150 CE: as long as a temple, carrying enough grain to feed Athens (Ἀθῆναι) for a year. Normally such ships sailed from Alexandria (Ἀλεξάνδρεια) to Rome (Roma), weeks at sea. Imagined: the sailors watch for the great lighthouse of Ostia, and when its fire appears on the horizon, they cheer.",
  "romans:festival": "At Saturnalia in December, Rome (Roma) turned upside down. Everyone wore the felt cap of freed slaves, masters served their slaves at table, people gambled openly and gave gifts of candles and small clay figures. The streets rang with the cry io Saturnalia! Seneca complained that the whole city was in a frenzy, and Pliny (Plinius) hid in a quiet room.",
  "romans:games": "Gaius Appuleius Diocles, a charioteer from Lusitania, raced for twenty-four years in the second century CE. His inscription records 4,257 races and 1,462 wins, and prize money worth a fortune. At the Circus Maximus, two hundred and fifty thousand spectators screamed for their colors, Reds (russata), Whites (albata), Greens (prasina) or Blues (veneta), and some even placed curses on rival drivers.",
  "romans:drought": "In 22 BCE, after floods and disease, famine struck Rome (Roma). The grain fleets were late, and the people crowded the city in hunger. Augustus took charge of the grain supply and paid for grain from his own funds. For ordinary Romans (Romani), the monthly grain dole was the line between life and hunger, and every delay was felt.",
  "romans:quake": "On August 24, 79 CE, Vesuvius erupted. Pliny the Younger (Plinius Minor), watching from across the bay at Misenum, described a cloud shaped like an umbrella pine. His uncle sailed toward the danger to rescue friends and died on the beach. Pompeii and Herculaneum were buried, with their bread in the ovens and graffiti on the walls, until they were found again in the 1700s.",
  "romans:winter": "Livy (Livius) records a terrible winter around 398 BCE: the roads were blocked by snow, and the Tiber (Tiberis) froze so hard it could not be sailed. Many cattle died, and the following year brought sickness. Imagined: families huddle around braziers in their small rooms, and children slide on the frozen river.",
  "romans:plague": "In 165 CE soldiers returning from the east brought a plague, now thought to be smallpox. The physician Galen (Γαληνός) described fever, black rash and diarrhea. It spread across the empire for years; in Rome (Roma) thousands died in a day. Families painted protective verses from Apollo on their doors, and Galen, trying to heal, wrote everything he saw.",
  "romans:wedding": "A Roman bride wears a flame-colored veil, the flammeum, and her hair is parted with a spearhead. The couple join right hands. In the evening, a procession leads her to her husband's house, with torches and flute music, as guests shout Talassio! and throw nuts. At the door she anoints the doorposts, and her husband carries her across the threshold.",

  // ── Carthaginians ──
  "carthaginians:siege": "Carthage (𐤒𐤓𐤕𐤇𐤃𐤔𐤕), 149 to 146 BCE. Under Roman siege, the people forge weapons day and night; women cut their hair to make ropes for the catapults. After three years, the Romans break into the city and fight house to house for six days. At the end, the wife of the commander, seeing her husband surrender, throws herself and her children into the fire of the burning temple.",
  "carthaginians:triumph": "After Cannae, in 216 BCE, Hannibal's (𐤇𐤍𐤁𐤏𐤋) brother Mago (𐤌𐤂𐤍) sailed home to Carthage (𐤒𐤓𐤕𐤇𐤃𐤔𐤕). In the council, he poured out a heap of gold rings taken from the fallen Roman knights, so many they were measured by the bushel. Imagined: the news runs through the harbor and the markets, and families of the soldiers abroad hold feasts, hoping their sons will come home.",
  "carthaginians:build": "The harbor of Carthage (𐤒𐤓𐤕𐤇𐤃𐤔𐤕) was a wonder: a rectangular merchant harbor, and beyond it a circular naval harbor with an island in the center. Appian (Ἀππιανός) describes ship sheds for 220 warships, each with columns, so it looked like a portico. Imagined: workers dig the basin and line it with stone, singing to keep time, while shipwrights wait to build the ships.",
  "carthaginians:voyage": "Around 500 BCE, Hanno (𐤇𐤍𐤀) sailed from Carthage (𐤒𐤓𐤕𐤇𐤃𐤔𐤕) with sixty ships beyond the Pillars of Heracles (Ἡρακλέους στῆλαι) and down the coast of West Africa to found colonies. His account tells of rivers full of crocodiles, a mountain burning at night that they called the Chariot of the Gods (Θεῶν Ὄχημα), and hairy wild people the interpreters called gorillas. The voyage went farther than any before.",
  "carthaginians:festival": "Imagined: on a festival day for Tanit (𐤕𐤍𐤕), lady of Carthage (𐤒𐤓𐤕𐤇𐤃𐤔𐤕), families climb to the temples with offerings of incense, fruit and small clay figures. Musicians play tambourines and double pipes, and merchants crowd the market near the harbor, selling purple cloth, glass beads and garum, while ships from every coast fill the docks.",
  "carthaginians:games": "Imagined: on the plains outside Carthage (𐤒𐤓𐤕𐤇𐤃𐤔𐤕), young men race horses, watched by Numidian horsemen who ride without bridles. Others wrestle in the dust, and crowds bet on the winners. Later, under Rome, Carthage built one of the largest circuses in Africa, and its people loved chariot racing as much as any city.",
  "carthaginians:drought": "Mago (𐤌𐤂𐤍) of Carthage (𐤒𐤓𐤕𐤇𐤃𐤔𐤕) wrote a famous book on farming, twenty-eight volumes long, so valued that the Roman senate had it translated into Latin. He taught how to plant vines and olives to survive dry summers, and how to choose land. Imagined: in a drought, farmers in the hills follow his advice, digging deep and saving every drop of water.",
  "carthaginians:quake": "Imagined: an earthquake shakes Carthage (𐤒𐤓𐤕𐤇𐤃𐤔𐤕) at night, and lamps fall in the houses on the Byrsa hill. In the morning, cracks run through the walls, and the people gather at the temples. Priests make offerings to Baal Hammon (𐤁𐤏𐤋 𐤇𐤌𐤍) and Tanit (𐤕𐤍𐤕), and the builders begin to repair the damage.",
  "carthaginians:winter": "In autumn 218 BCE, Hannibal's (𐤇𐤍𐤁𐤏𐤋) army crossed the Alps (Alpes). Snow fell, the paths were icy, and men and elephants slipped over the cliffs. Livy (Livius) says the soldiers broke rocks by heating them with fire and pouring vinegar on them. Polybius (Πολύβιος) writes how Hannibal showed the men the plains of Italy (Italia) below, to give them courage.",
  "carthaginians:plague": "In 396 BCE, a Carthaginian army besieging Syracuse (Συράκουσαι) was struck by plague in the summer heat, Diodorus (Διόδωρος) writes. The soldiers died in their camp, unable to bury their dead. The few who returned to Carthage (𐤒𐤓𐤕𐤇𐤃𐤔𐤕) brought grief to the whole city, and people closed their shops and wept in the streets.",
  "carthaginians:wedding": "Imagined: in Carthage (𐤒𐤓𐤕𐤇𐤃𐤔𐤕), the bride, adorned with gold earrings and necklaces of glass beads made in the city's workshops, is led to her husband's house. The families feast on fish, figs and wine, and musicians play. Small amulets and clay masks were placed to protect the new home from evil.",

  // ── Aztecs (no winter) ──
  "aztecs:siege": "Tenochtitlan (Tenōchtitlan), 1521. For eighty days the island city is surrounded by Spanish ships and tens of thousands of native allies. Smallpox has already killed many. A song written soon after says: broken spears lie in the roads, we have torn our hair in grief, we have chewed dry twigs and salt grasses. The Mexica (Mēxihcah) hold out until the end, defending Tlatelolco (Tlatelōlco) street by street.",
  "aztecs:triumph": "Imagined: the warriors return to Tenochtitlan (Tenōchtitlan) from a campaign. Those who took captives are honored with new mantles and the right to wear certain ornaments, and their calpulli (calpōlli), their neighborhood, celebrates with songs and drums. Mothers and wives welcome them at the causeways, and the old men tell the young ones about their own campaigns.",
  "aztecs:build": "In 1449, after a terrible flood, the poet king Nezahualcoyotl (Nezahualcoyōtl) of Texcoco (Tetzcohco) designed a dike across Lake Texcoco, more than fifteen kilometers long, to keep the salt water away from Tenochtitlan's (Tenōchtitlan) fresh water and chinampa gardens. Thousands of workers from many cities drove stakes and filled them with stone and earth. Imagined: work songs rise across the lake.",
  "aztecs:voyage": "The Mexica (Mēxihcah) told how their ancestors left Aztlan (Aztlān), a land of herons, and wandered for generations, guided by their god Huitzilopochtli. They would settle where they saw an eagle on a cactus. In 1325, on a small island in the lake, they saw it. There they founded Tenochtitlan (Tenōchtitlan). The eagle on the cactus is still a symbol of the land.",
  "aztecs:festival": "Every fifty-two years, when the two calendars came back to the same day, the Mexica (Mēxihcah) feared the world might end. All fires were put out. At night, priests climbed the hill of Huixachtlan and watched the Pleiades (tianquiztli). When the stars passed overhead, a new fire was kindled and carried by runners to every town. The last was in 1507.",
  "aztecs:games": "The ball game ollamaliztli (ōllamaliztli) was played with a heavy rubber ball, struck with the hips. Players scored by passing the ball through a stone ring high on the wall, rarely achieved. Spectators bet cloaks, jewels and even their homes. Patolli (Patōlli), a game of beans as dice, was so loved that people played it everywhere. Players carried their mats to the market.",
  "aztecs:drought": "In the year One Rabbit (Ce Tōchtli), 1454, the rains failed for the third year, frost had killed the maize, and the Mexica (Mēxihcah) starved. People sold themselves or their children to the Totonacs of the coast, where there was maize. After this, the Mexica feared any year One Rabbit. The kings built granaries to store maize for the bad years.",
  "aztecs:quake": "Aztec codices record earthquakes, tlalolin (tlālolīn), and the Mexica (Mēxihcah) believed this present world would end in an earthquake. The day sign Ollin (Ōlīn), movement, was sacred to this age. Imagined: when the ground shakes in Tenochtitlan (Tenōchtitlan), people run out of their houses, and the old men remember the omens and pray that it is not yet the end.",
  "aztecs:plague": "In 1520, smallpox swept through Tenochtitlan (Tenōchtitlan). The Florentine Codex, written down from Nahua (Nāhuah) survivors, says sores covered people's faces and bodies; they could not move or turn over, and many died of hunger because no one could care for them. They called it huey zahuatl (huēyi zahuatl), the great rash. It came just as the city was fighting for its life.",
  "aztecs:wedding": "The Florentine Codex describes a Nahua (Nāhuah) wedding: old women, the matchmakers, arrange it with the families. In the evening the bride is carried to the groom's house on the back of a woman, with torches. The couple sits on a mat by the hearth, and the matchmakers tie the corner of his cloak to her blouse. The elders give long speeches of advice.",

  // ── Mayans (no winter) ──
  "mayans:siege": "Dos Pilas, in the 760s CE. Under threat, the people tore stones from their own palaces and temples to build walls and wooden palisades around the center of the city, even across the royal plaza. Archaeologists found the hastily built walls. Imagined: families crowd inside, carrying maize, while the warriors watch the forest for the enemy.",
  "mayans:triumph": "The murals of Bonampak, painted around 790 CE, show a celebration: musicians shake rattles, beat drums and turtle shells, blow long trumpets, and dancers wear great feathered headdresses. Nobles in white mantles watch. The paintings show a whole festival, with a victory and a royal heir. The colors are still bright after twelve hundred years.",
  "mayans:build": "Imagined: in Tikal (Yax Mutul), workers carry limestone blocks up the temple steps, and others burn limestone in great fires to make lime plaster for the white and red walls. Each temple needed huge amounts of wood for the lime, and the forest around the city shrank. Masons carve glyphs into the stone, telling the story of the king.",
  "mayans:voyage": "In 1502, off the coast of Honduras, Columbus's ships met a great Maya trading canoe, carved from a single tree, as long as a galley, with twenty-five paddlers and a shelter in the middle. It carried cotton cloth, copper bells, obsidian blades and cacao beans, which the Maya used as money. Ferdinand Columbus noted how carefully they picked up dropped cacao.",
  "mayans:festival": "At the end of the 365-day year came Wayeb, five unlucky days. Bishop Landa wrote that people did not wash, comb their hair or do any hard work, fearing misfortune. Then the new year began with festivities, and the people cleaned their houses and threw out old pots and mats.",
  "mayans:games": "In the Popol Vuh (Popol Wuj), the hero twins Hunahpu (Junajpu) and Xbalanque (Xb'alanke) play the ball game against the lords of the underworld, Xibalba (Xib'alb'a), and win by cleverness. Maya cities built stone ball courts with sloping walls. Players struck the heavy rubber ball with their hips, and the crowds watched from the walls. The game still survives as ulama.",
  "mayans:drought": "Lake sediments show that between about 800 and 1000 CE, a series of severe droughts struck the Maya lowlands. Cities like Tikal (Yax Mutul) and Calakmul emptied. Imagined: farmers watch their maize wither, the reservoirs dry up, and families leave their houses and walk north or toward the rivers, carrying what they can.",
  "mayans:quake": "Around 600 CE, the volcano Loma Caldera erupted near the village of Joya de Cerén in El Salvador. The villagers fled, leaving their dinner bowls, their tools and even a duck tied in a corner. Ash buried the village so completely that it was preserved, the only Maya farming village where we can see daily life just as it was.",
  "mayans:plague": "The Annals of the Kaqchikels remember the sickness of the 1520s: great was the stench of the dead. After our fathers and grandfathers died, half the people fled into the fields. The dogs and vultures devoured the bodies. We were born to die, my children. The writer survived as a child and wrote it down for the next generation.",
  "mayans:wedding": "Bishop Landa wrote that Maya fathers chose wives for their sons with the help of matchmakers. The groom would serve his father-in-law for several years. On the wedding day, the families gathered in the bride's father's house, and the priest spoke to the couple. Imagined: the guests share tamales and cacao.",

  // ── Incas ──
  "incas:siege": "Vilcabamba (Willkapampa), in the hot forests below the Andes. For thirty-five years after 1537, a last group of Incas (Inka) held out in this remote valley, with their own temples and houses. In 1572 an army came, and the people set fire to the town and fled into the forest. Tupac Amaru (Thupa Amaru) was captured. The people remembered Vilcabamba as their last refuge.",
  "incas:triumph": "After a victory, the Inca (Inka) armies returned to Cusco (Qusqu) in procession, and people sang the haylli, songs of triumph. Chroniclers describe the songs being sung at harvest and in war. Imagined: the soldiers march into the great plaza, the conch trumpets sound, and the families sing haylli and dance in circles.",
  "incas:build": "Every year, four Quechua (Runa Simi) communities near the Apurimac (Apurimaq) river rebuild the Q'eswachaka bridge, the last Inca (Inka) rope bridge. Families twist ichu grass into cords, the cords into great cables, and men weave the new bridge across the gorge in three days, while women sing. The old bridge is let fall into the river.",
  "incas:voyage": "The chronicler Sarmiento told a legend that the Inca Tupac Yupanqui (Inka Thupa Yupanki) sailed with an army on balsa rafts into the Pacific, and returned months later with captives and treasure from distant islands. No one knows if it is true. Imagined: the rafts drift west for weeks until a thin line of land appears on the horizon.",
  "incas:festival": "Inti Raymi, at the June solstice, celebrated the sun. In Cusco (Qusqu) the people fasted for days, then gathered in the great square before dawn, barefoot, waiting for the first light. When the sun rose, they greeted it with song and offered chicha. Today Inti Raymi is again celebrated at Sacsayhuaman (Saksaywaman).",
  "incas:games": "Imagined: in a village in the highlands, young men race up the mountain paths, practicing to become chasqui (chaski) runners, who carried messages across the empire in relays, about 240 kilometers a day. Others play pichqa, a game with a five-sided die. The winners are cheered and given chicha.",
  "incas:drought": "The Incas (Inka) built thousands of storehouses, qollqa, on hillsides, filled with freeze-dried potatoes, maize and cloth. In a drought, the storehouses fed the people. Imagined: in a village where the rain has failed, the officials open the qollqa, and the families carry home sacks of chuño (ch'uñu), grateful.",
  "incas:quake": "In 1650 a great earthquake shook Cusco (Qusqu) and destroyed most of the colonial churches and houses, but the Inca (Inka) walls, made of fitted stones without mortar, stood firm. Inca builders had designed walls that sloped inward and trapezoidal doors. The stones shake and settle, but do not fall.",
  "incas:winter": "In June and July, the dry winter of the Andes brings freezing nights. Families lay potatoes on the ground to freeze at night and thaw in the sun, then trample them with bare feet to squeeze out the water. The result is chuño (ch'uñu), which can be stored for years. Imagined: families work together on the frosty fields at dawn.",
  "incas:plague": "Around 1527, before any Spaniard reached Cusco (Qusqu), smallpox arrived from the north. The Inca Huayna Capac (Inka Wayna Qhapaq) died of it in Quito (Kitu), and so did his heir. Chroniclers say the sickness killed many across the Andes. For the people, it was a terror without a name, and the empire was left in grief and confusion.",
  "incas:wedding": "Andean couples often lived together first in a trial marriage, sirvinakuy, still practiced in some villages. Chroniclers say that in Inca (Inka) times, local officials married young couples in group ceremonies. Imagined: the families exchange gifts of cloth and food, and the village dances with flutes and drums.",

  // ── Haudenosaunee ──
  "haudenosaunee:siege": "Imagined: a Haudenosaunee (Rotinonhsión:ni) town stands on a hill above a river, ringed by a double palisade of sharpened logs with platforms for the defenders. Enemies have camped around it for days. Women carry water and stones up to the platforms, children help bring arrows, and the corn in the storage pits keeps everyone fed. At night the defenders sing to keep their spirits strong.",
  "haudenosaunee:triumph": "Imagined: the young men return to the town after a long journey, calling out from the edge of the forest so the people know who comes home. The clan mothers count them. That night there are speeches, a feast of corn soup and venison, and social dances in the longhouse, with water drums and horn rattles keeping time.",
  "haudenosaunee:build": "A longhouse could be sixty meters long, home to several families of one clan. Neighbors gather to set the bent poles of ironwood and cover them with sheets of elm bark peeled in spring. Imagined: the men lift the poles while the women tie the bark with basswood cords, and an older man sings to keep the work moving, laughter rising with the rafters.",
  "haudenosaunee:voyage": "Haudenosaunee tradition tells how the Peacemaker came across Lake Ontario in a canoe of white stone, bringing a message of peace to nations at war. With Hiawatha (Haiëñ'wa'tha) he traveled from town to town, until the five nations planted the Tree of Peace. The story is told at gatherings, and the canoe of white stone is never forgotten.",
  "haudenosaunee:festival": "In early summer, when the wild strawberries ripen, Haudenosaunee communities hold a thanksgiving for them. Strawberry juice is shared by everyone, because the strawberry is said to grow on the path to the Creator's land. Before such gatherings the Thanksgiving Address (Ganǫhonyohk) is spoken, greeting the people, the earth, the waters, the plants and the sky in turn.",
  "haudenosaunee:games": "Lacrosse is the Creator's game, Dehontsigwaehs, played to give thanks, to heal and to settle disputes. Teams of whole towns once played on fields a mile long. Players carry wooden sticks of bent hickory with deer gut nets. Imagined: the elders watch from the side, the young men run until they fall, and the crowd shouts across the field.",
  "haudenosaunee:drought": "Haudenosaunee (Rotinonhsión:ni) life rests on the Three Sisters: corn, beans and squash grown together. Imagined: in a dry summer, the corn leaves curl, and the women who own the fields carry water from the river in bark buckets. Families ration the stored corn, and the people ask the Thunderers (Ratiwé:ras), the grandfathers in the west, to bring rain.",
  "haudenosaunee:quake": "In February 1663 a great earthquake shook the whole St. Lawrence valley and the lands to the south. The Jesuit Relations describe forests shaking, rivers changing their course, and people running from their houses. Imagined: in a Haudenosaunee (Rotinonhsión:ni) town, the longhouse poles creak and sway, and the elders gather to talk about what the earth is saying.",
  "haudenosaunee:winter": "The winter of 1779 to 1780 was one of the hardest anyone remembered. After their towns and orchards had been burned in the autumn, thousands of Haudenosaunee (Rotinonhsión:ni) families camped near Niagara in deep snow. Food was scarce, many people died of cold and hunger, and the animals froze in the woods. The elders remembered it as the winter the snow lay waist deep.",
  "haudenosaunee:plague": "In the 1630s and 1640s, smallpox and measles swept through the towns of the Haudenosaunee (Rotinonhsión:ni), killing perhaps half the people. The Jesuit Relations describe longhouses where whole families lay ill. Clans that lost many members adopted others into their families to carry on the names. Mourning and condolence rituals helped the survivors bear the loss.",
  "haudenosaunee:wedding": "In the old way described by nineteenth-century observers, the mothers arranged a marriage. The bride brought a basket of corn bread to the groom's mother, and the groom's mother sent back venison or other game, showing that each family would provide. The couple lived in the longhouse of the wife's clan, and the children belonged to the mother's clan.",

  // ── Lakota (no flood) ──
  "lakota:siege": "In the winter after the summer of 1876, soldiers followed the Lakota (Lakȟóta) camps across the snow. Families moved again and again, with little time to hunt. In January 1877, at the Wolf Mountains, the people fought in a blizzard to cover the women and children as they escaped. Through the cold months they held out, hungry, horses thin, until in spring many bands came in to the agencies.",
  "lakota:triumph": "In June 1876, after the fight on the Greasy Grass (Pȟežísla Wakpá), the Little Bighorn, the camps along the river held victory dances through the night. Black Elk (Heȟáka Sápa), then a boy of twelve, remembered the drums and the songs, and how the women sang the names of the brave. Families also mourned their own dead. Then the great camp broke up and moved on.",
  "lakota:build": "The tipi (thípi) belonged to the woman, who made and raised it. Women sewed the cover from tanned buffalo hides, sometimes with a feast for the helpers. Raising it, they tied three or four main poles, then leaned the others in a cone, lifted the cover with the last pole, and pinned it closed. Imagined: two women raise a tipi in minutes, talking and laughing.",
  "lakota:voyage": "In the 1700s the Lakota (Lakȟóta) moved west from the woodlands near the Mississippi onto the open plains, following the buffalo and learning to ride horses. A winter count records that around 1776 Standing Bull reached the Black Hills, Ȟe Sápa, and brought back a pine branch. Imagined: the families see the dark hills rising from the plain for the first time.",
  "lakota:festival": "In summer, Lakota (Lakȟóta) bands gathered in great camps after the long winter apart. There were feasts, giveaways, horse races and social dances, wačhípi, where families met, young people courted, and news was shared. Some ceremonies of the gathering are sacred and kept by the people themselves. For the families, it was the happiest time of the year.",
  "lakota:games": "In summer camps, young men raced horses on the prairie while the people bet on them. Boys played the hoop and pole game, throwing darts at a rolling hoop. Women played shinny, a game with curved sticks and a ball, and girls played a game with plum seeds as dice. Imagined: shouts and laughter fill the camp as the riders cross the finish line.",
  "lakota:drought": "Imagined: in a dry summer, the prairie grass turns yellow, the creeks dry up, and the buffalo move far away. The scouts ride for days without finding a herd. The families eat dried meat and wild turnips, tȟíŋpsiŋla, and the camp moves again and again in search of water and game, praying for rain.",
  "lakota:quake": "Earthquakes are rare on the northern plains. Imagined: one summer night the ground shakes, the tipi poles rattle, and the horses scream and break loose. In the morning the elders talk about what it means, and the young men go out to gather the scattered horses. The keeper of the winter count may choose this as the picture for the year.",
  "lakota:winter": "Lakota (Lakȟóta) keepers painted winter counts, waníyetu wówapi, one picture for each year. Many years are remembered by their cold: the winter of deep snow, the winter many horses froze. Imagined: the camp shelters in a wooded river valley, the snow piled around the tipis, families sharing their dried meat and telling stories by the fire until spring.",
  "lakota:plague": "In 1837, smallpox came up the Missouri river on a steamboat and spread across the plains. Lakota (Lakȟóta) winter counts mark the year with figures covered in spots. Whole camps fell sick, and many families were lost. Some Lakota bands had been vaccinated a few years earlier and suffered less. The survivors grieved and moved on.",
  "lakota:wedding": "A young man courted with a cedar flute, siyótȟaŋka, playing at the edge of the camp at night, and stood with the girl under his blanket near her tipi. To marry, he brought horses as gifts to her family. Her family answered with gifts in return, and the couple's new tipi was made by the women of her family.",

  // ── Americans ──
  "americans:siege": "Vicksburg, Mississippi, 1863. For forty-seven days the town is surrounded and shelled day and night. Families dig caves into the clay hills and live underground with their furniture and children. Flour runs out; people eat mule meat and rats. On July 4 the town surrenders. One woman wrote in her diary of the silence after the guns stopped.",
  "americans:triumph": "August 14, 1945. When the news came that the war was over, crowds poured into Times Square in New York, cheering, kissing strangers, throwing paper from office windows. A sailor kissed a nurse, and the photograph became famous. In small towns, church bells rang and car horns blared. Families waited for the soldiers to come home.",
  "americans:build": "In the 1870s, workers dug the Big Bend Tunnel in West Virginia for the railroad. Legend says John Henry, a powerful steel driver, raced a steam drill with his hammer and won, but died with his hammer in his hand. Railroad workers sang his ballad to keep time as their hammers struck the steel.",
  "americans:voyage": "In September 1620 the Mayflower left Plymouth with 102 passengers. For sixty-six days they crossed the Atlantic, through storms that cracked a main beam. On November 9 they sighted the sandy hills of Cape Cod. William Bradford wrote that they fell on their knees and blessed God who had brought them over the vast and furious ocean.",
  "americans:festival": "Every December 31 since 1907, a ball has dropped from a pole above Times Square in New York at midnight. Crowds count down the last seconds, and then the confetti flies, horns blow, and strangers hug while the band plays Auld Lang Syne. Across the country, families watch on television and make resolutions for the new year.",
  "americans:games": "July 4, 1939, Yankee Stadium. Lou Gehrig, the Iron Horse, who had played 2,130 games in a row, stood before sixty thousand fans after his illness ended his career. Today, he said, I consider myself the luckiest man on the face of the earth. The stadium fell silent, and then cheered for minutes.",
  "americans:drought": "April 14, 1935, Black Sunday. A wall of dust rolls across the southern plains, turning day into night. After years of drought, the topsoil of Kansas, Oklahoma and Texas blows away. Families stuff wet rags around the windows and hold their children close. Woody Guthrie wrote So Long, It's Been Good to Know Yuh about it, and many families packed up for California.",
  "americans:quake": "April 18, 1906, early morning. An earthquake shakes San Francisco for nearly a minute. Houses collapse, and fires break out across the city, burning for three days. Families camp in Golden Gate Park, cooking in the street. People wrote their addresses on the walls of the ruins so relatives could find them.",
  "americans:winter": "January 12, 1888. A warm morning on the prairies of Nebraska and Dakota, children walk to school without coats. In the afternoon a blizzard strikes suddenly, with wind and snow so thick no one can see. Many children were lost trying to get home. It became known as the Children's Blizzard, and the families never forgot it.",
  "americans:plague": "In autumn 1918, the influenza epidemic swept the country. Schools, churches and theaters closed. People wore gauze masks in the streets. In Philadelphia, after a parade, hundreds died each day. Carpenters could not make coffins fast enough. Families nursed their own, and children skipped rope to a rhyme: I had a little bird, its name was Enza.",
  "americans:wedding": "Imagined: in a small town, the bride wears something old, something new, something borrowed, something blue. After the church, the families gather in a barn decorated with lights, and a fiddler plays square dances. In many African American families, the couple jumps the broom, a tradition remembered from the days of slavery, to the cheers of the guests.",

  // ── Mexicans ──
  "mexicans:siege": "Imagined: during the Revolution, around 1914, a town in the north is surrounded for weeks. The soldaderas, women who followed the troops, cook tortillas over small fires and carry water under fire. Families hide in church cellars. When it is over, a singer composes a corrido about the siege, and it is sung in markets for years.",
  "mexicans:triumph": "Imagined: in a village in the mountains, the men return from the fighting. The church bells ring, a brass band plays, and women throw flowers. Families prepare mole and tamales for the whole village. That evening, by lantern light, a singer with a guitar sings a new corrido about their bravery.",
  "mexicans:build": "In the villages of Oaxaca, the tequio is the tradition of communal work: every family sends someone to build the school, repair the church, or clear the road, without pay. Imagined: on a Sunday, the whole village carries stones and mixes mortar, the women bring food, and a band plays as the walls rise.",
  "mexicans:voyage": "For 250 years, from 1565 to 1815, the Manila galleon, the Nao de China, crossed the Pacific to Acapulco, a voyage of four to six months. When the ship was sighted, the town filled with merchants for the great fair, buying silk, porcelain and spices. Imagined: sailors weak from scurvy weep at the sight of the Mexican coast.",
  "mexicans:festival": "From December 16 to 24, Las Posadas recall Mary (María) and Joseph's (José) search for shelter. Each night, neighbors walk with candles, singing at doors, asking for posada. Those inside refuse, until finally they open the door, and everyone enters for tamales, ponche and a piñata. Children break the star-shaped piñata and scramble for the candy.",
  "mexicans:games": "In 1942, a masked wrestler in silver entered the ring in Mexico City (Ciudad de México): El Santo. For decades he fought in lucha libre, never removing his mask in public, and starred in films. Lucha libre is a family spectacle: fans cheer the técnicos, boo the rudos, and children buy masks of their heroes.",
  "mexicans:drought": "In 1785, early frost killed the maize in central Mexico, and drought followed. The year 1786 became known as el año del hambre, the year of hunger. Thousands died, and people left their villages looking for food. The church and the towns opened granaries, and families ate roots and herbs.",
  "mexicans:quake": "September 19, 1985, 7:19 in the morning. An earthquake strikes Mexico City (Ciudad de México), and buildings collapse. Thousands die. Ordinary people dig through the rubble with their hands, forming brigades. The volunteers became known as topos, moles. For days, they worked to find survivors, sometimes calling for silence to hear voices under the stones.",
  "mexicans:winter": "On January 11, 1967, snow fell on Mexico City (Ciudad de México), something almost no one alive had seen. Children ran outside and made snowmen in the streets, people threw snowballs in the Zócalo, and photographers rushed to capture the white volcanoes and white rooftops. The city remembers it as a day of wonder.",
  "mexicans:plague": "In 1737, an epidemic called matlazahuatl swept through Mexico City (Ciudad de México), killing thousands. The city declared the Virgin of Guadalupe (Virgen de Guadalupe) its patron, praying for her protection, and the epidemic slowly ended. Families lit candles in their homes and processions filled the streets. The devotion of that year remains in the city's memory.",
  "mexicans:wedding": "At a Mexican wedding, padrinos, godparents, support the couple. During the Mass, a lazo, a rope of flowers or rosary beads, is placed around the couple's shoulders, and the groom gives the bride thirteen gold coins, arras. At the party, mariachis play, and the guests dance la víbora de la mar, running under the arms of the couple.",

  // ── Mapuche ──
  "mapuche:siege": "Imagined: a Mapuche community has withdrawn to a pukara, a hilltop fort ringed with ditches and wooden stakes. Enemy riders circle below. Inside, families share stored potatoes and dried meat, and the women grind maize while the weichafe, the warriors, watch. At night, the kultrun (kultrung) drum sounds to keep the people's courage.",
  "mapuche:triumph": "Imagined: the weichafe return home on horseback, and the community gathers. The trutruka, a long horn of cane, blows, and the kultrun (kultrung) beats. Women prepare muday, a drink of fermented maize or wheat, and the families feast. The elders speak of the warriors' bravery, and the young people dance.",
  "mapuche:build": "When a family needs a new ruka, the traditional house of straw and wood, the neighbors come for a mingako, a day of shared work. They set the posts, weave the walls and thatch the roof with straw and reeds. When the work is done, the host family offers a great meal of meat, bread and muday to all.",
  "mapuche:voyage": "From the 1600s on, Mapuche families crossed the Andes through the mountain passes to the east, to Puelmapu, the land of the east, the great Pampas. They drove horses and cattle, and traded. Imagined: after days in the snow of the passes, the families see the plains open below, and give thanks.",
  "mapuche:festival": "We Tripantu, the Mapuche new year, falls at the winter solstice in June. Families gather the night before, sharing food and stories. At dawn, the young people bathe in the cold river to receive the new year's strength. Then the families welcome the sun's return as the days begin to lengthen.",
  "mapuche:games": "Palin, the Mapuche game played with curved sticks and a small ball, can last all day. Communities challenge each other, the players paint their faces, and the families come to watch and cheer. After the game, the two communities share a feast. Palin is still played today.",
  "mapuche:drought": "Imagined: the rains fail in the south, and the streams dry up. The wheat and potatoes wither. The families carry water from the river and share their stored food. The community comes together and asks the lonko, the chief, and the elders to call the people to pray for rain.",
  "mapuche:quake": "On May 22, 1960, the strongest earthquake ever recorded struck the south of Chile, near Valdivia, followed by a tsunami. Mapuche communities lost their homes. Mapuche tradition tells of the serpents Tren Tren (Trengtreng) and Kai Kai (Kaykay): Kai Kai raised the waters, and Tren Tren raised the mountains to save the people.",
  "mapuche:winter": "In the southern Andes, the Pewenche, people of the pewen tree, gather the seeds of the araucaria in autumn and store them for winter. Imagined: snow covers the mountains, families gather around the fire in the ruka, roasting the seeds and telling stories, and the children listen to the wind in the trees.",
  "mapuche:plague": "Imagined: in the 1500s, a sickness never seen before comes with the strangers. The Mapuche call it chavalongo, the fever. Families fall ill, and the machi, the healers, use herbs and prayers. Many die, and the communities that survive mourn their dead.",
  "mapuche:wedding": "In the traditional Mapuche wedding, the groom and his friends would ride to the bride's home and take her away in a ritual chase, the ñapitun, with her consent. Then the groom's family pays gifts, the mafün, to the bride's family, and both families feast together.",

  // ── Muisca (no winter) ──
  "muisca:siege": "Imagined: the Muisca (Muysca) villages of the high plains are protected by cercados, enclosures of wooden posts. When enemies approach, the people retreat inside, bringing maize and potatoes. Warriors stand at the gates, and the women prepare chicha (fapqua). The siege lasts days, and the people hold out until the enemy withdraws.",
  "muisca:triumph": "Imagined: the güechas, the Muisca (Muysca) warriors who guarded the frontier against the Panche, come home up the mountain paths to the high plain of Bacatá. The people meet them with gourds of chicha (fapqua), songs and long dances to flutes and drums. The warriors wear their feathers and gold ornaments, and the old men tell the children how each one fought.",
  "muisca:build": "In Sugamuxi, the sacred city of the sun, the Muisca (Muysca) built a temple with great wooden columns brought from far away. Chroniclers say it burned in 1537 when strangers carrying torches entered it. Imagined: the builders drag the great posts across the high plain, singing to keep time.",
  "muisca:voyage": "The Muisca (Muysca) traded salt from the mines of Zipaquirá and Nemocón, and emeralds, with neighboring peoples for cotton, gold and feathers. Imagined: a group of traders walks down from the high plains with salt cakes, crossing mountains and rivers, until they see the warm valleys below.",
  "muisca:festival": "Imagined: at harvest, the Muisca (Muysca) of the high plains gather for a festival. They brew chicha (fapqua), cook maize and potatoes, and dance to flutes and drums. Chroniclers described processions of dancers in costumes and masks, singing for days. The festival marks the end of the hard work.",
  "muisca:games": "The game of tejo, played in Colombia today, is said to descend from a Muisca (Muysca) game of throwing a metal or stone disk at a target. Imagined: young Muisca men gather in the evening, throwing the disk at a target, betting and laughing, sharing chicha (fapqua).",
  "muisca:drought": "Imagined: the rains do not come to the high plain, and the maize and potatoes wither in the raised fields. The lakes and marshes shrink, and the reeds turn brown. Families walk to the sacred lakes to leave offerings, as their parents did, and share what is left in their storehouses, waiting for the clouds to gather again over the mountains.",
  "muisca:quake": "Imagined: the earth shakes beneath the high plain of the Muisca (Muysca), and the round houses of wood, cane and straw sway and creak. People run outside into the fields with their children, and the dogs howl. Afterward the elders speak about what the earth means by it, and neighbors help one another raise the fallen posts and tie the roofs back on.",
  "muisca:plague": "In the decades after 1537, waves of smallpox and other new diseases reached the high plains, and Spanish records speak of a great epidemic in 1558. Imagined: in a Muisca (Muysca) village, whole families fall ill at once, and there are too few hands to bring in the maize. The healers try herbs and chants, and the survivors mourn the elders who carried the old knowledge.",
  "muisca:wedding": "Imagined: a Muisca (Muysca) wedding on the high plain. The groom's family brings gifts of painted cotton mantles, the fine cloth the Muisca were known for, and the bride's family answers with food and chicha (fapqua). The couple sit together before the elders, who give them advice for married life. Then the whole village drinks, sings and dances to flutes and drums until morning.",

  // ── Tupi (no winter) ──
  "tupi:siege": "Hans Staden, a German held captive by the Tupinambá in the 1550s, described their villages protected by double palisades of logs. Imagined: an enemy war party surrounds the village, and the defenders shoot arrows from behind the palisade, while women bring water and food. They hold out until the enemy withdraws.",
  "tupi:triumph": "Jean de Léry described how the Tupinambá warriors returned from war and the village celebrated with cauim, a drink of fermented manioc, and dances that lasted for days. The warriors recounted their deeds, and the women sang. Imagined: the drums beat as the people celebrate.",
  "tupi:build": "The Tupinambá lived in great longhouses, oca, each home to many families. The whole village worked together to build them, cutting poles and thatching roofs with palm leaves. Imagined: the men climb the frame, the women pass up the leaves, and the children run between them, laughing.",
  "tupi:voyage": "The Tupi-Guarani peoples migrated across South America in search of Yvy Marã'ey, the Land without Evil, a place without death or suffering. Guided by their shamans, whole villages left their homes and walked for months. Imagined: the people reach the sea and see the endless water stretching before them.",
  "tupi:festival": "In 1557 Jean de Léry heard the Tupinambá singing in a great house: men singing in chorus, Heu, heuraure, heura. He wrote that he was so moved that whenever he remembered it his heart trembled. Imagined: the dance continues through the night, with maraca (mbaraká) rattles and cauim.",
  "tupi:games": "Imagined: on a bright afternoon the young men of a Tupi village race each other into the river, swimming against the current, while others shoot arrows at a target of woven palm. Tupi boys learned to shoot fish from canoes almost as soon as they could walk. The winners are praised in song, and the whole village shares cauim and dances into the night.",
  "tupi:drought": "Imagined: the rains fail along the coast, the streams shrink to pools, and the manioc in the burned fields wilts. Fish grow scarce in the low rivers. The village decides to move deeper into the forest, where the water still runs, carrying hammocks, pots and baskets of farinha. The elders lead the way, and the children carry the parrots on their shoulders.",
  "tupi:quake": "Imagined: one night the ground trembles beneath a Tupi village, something almost never felt on that coast. The hammocks swing on their posts, gourds fall, and the longhouse frame creaks above the sleeping families. Everyone runs out into the clearing. In the morning the pajé, the healer and seer, is asked what it means, and the people gather to listen.",
  "tupi:plague": "In 1562 and 1563, smallpox swept through the Tupinambá villages of Bahia, killing tens of thousands, and famine followed because no one was strong enough to plant. Jesuit letters describe the dead lying unburied and families fleeing into the forest, some selling themselves for food. Whole villages disappeared, and the survivors carried the grief with them wherever they went.",
  "tupi:wedding": "Jean de Léry wrote that among the Tupinambá, a young man who wished to marry asked the girl's father, and if he agreed, the marriage was made. The son-in-law would then hunt, fish and clear fields for her family. Imagined: the families gather in the longhouse, the bride painted with genipapo and wearing feathers, and the village celebrates with cauim and dancing.",
});
